insert into public.questions
  (id, module, domain, skill, difficulty, passage, prompt, choices, answer, explanation)
values
  ($q$ii-h76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the poem "Winter Field" by Tomas Linde, the speaker finds unexpected beauty in a landscape that most people would consider bleak.$q$, $q$Which quotation from "Winter Field" most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"The field is bare, the fences gray, / and yet the frost on every stalk / holds the low sun like a jewel / that no one else has stopped to see.\"","\"I crossed the field in early March / and longed for green, for any green, / for any sign the cold would end / and give the farmers back their land.\"","\"The neighbors say the field is cursed, / that nothing grows there anymore; / they hurry past it after dark / and do not look across the snow.\"","\"In summer, wheat stood shoulder high / and larks rose singing from the rows; / the field was gold from fence to fence / and everyone stopped by to see.\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The first quotation acknowledges the bleakness ("bare," "gray") and finds beauty in the frost. The others express longing, others' fear, or summer beauty.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Agricultural scientist Grace Wu hypothesizes that planting clover between rows of corn increases corn yields by adding nitrogen to the soil.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Wu's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Corn grown with clover was taller in early summer and produced higher yields at harvest than corn grown alone in the same fields.","Clover planted between rows of corn reduced the number of weeds, which compete with corn for nitrogen and other nutrients in the soil.","Farmers who planted clover between rows of corn spent less on herbicides.","Corn grown with clover produced higher yields than corn grown alone, but only in fields where soil nitrogen was initially low."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Yield gains only where nitrogen was low point to nitrogen as the mechanism. Weed suppression offers a different mechanism.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a particular bird species migrates by following Earth's magnetic field rather than by using the positions of the stars.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["Birds of the species migrate mostly at night and become noticeably disoriented on cloudy nights, when the stars are hidden from view.","Birds of the species follow the same routes each year and stop to rest at the same sites along the way.","In a planetarium, birds ignored rotated stars and kept their usual heading, but turned when a surrounding magnetic field was shifted.","Young birds raised indoors under an artificial night sky oriented correctly only after the artificial stars were switched on."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ignoring rotated stars but responding to shifted magnetic fields isolates the magnetic cue.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Historian Daniel Moss argues that a nineteenth-century novelist based her depiction of factory life on firsthand observation rather than on secondhand accounts.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Moss's argument?$q$, $q$["The novel was widely read by factory owners when it was published, and several of them praised its accurate portrayal of their mills.","The novelist's diaries record visits to textile mills, with details of machines and workers' talk that reappear almost verbatim in the novel.","Many other novels of the period depicted factory life, often relying on newspaper reports of conditions in the mills.","The novelist was born in a city with many textile mills and lived there until she was in her twenties."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Diary entries matching the novel's details directly show firsthand observation.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A nutrition researcher claims that eating breakfast improves students' concentration in morning classes.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["Students who were randomly assigned to eat breakfast performed no better on morning attention tests than students who were randomly assigned to skip it.","Students who eat breakfast tend to score higher on morning attention tests than students who skip it, but they also tend to sleep more.","Many students say that they feel hungry and have trouble focusing during morning classes on days when they skip breakfast.","Schools that serve free breakfast to all students report fewer students visiting the nurse with headaches in the morning."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A randomized comparison finding no difference directly undercuts the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the novel The Glass Coast by Rina Patel, the protagonist, a young fisherman named Arun, is portrayed as torn between loyalty to his family and his desire to leave the village.$q$, $q$Which quotation from The Glass Coast most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"Arun was the best swimmer in the village, and his father often said that the sea itself had chosen him to be a fisherman.\"","\"Arun had no interest in the city; he loved the sea and could not imagine living anywhere else.\"","\"Arun's father taught him to read the weather from the clouds over the bay, and Arun never once doubted his father's judgment.\"","\"Each evening Arun mended nets beside his father, and each evening he watched the city bus pull away without him.\""]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Mending nets with his father while watching the city bus leave captures both loyalty and longing.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Hassan Diallo argues that the decline of a frog species in a mountain region was caused primarily by a fungal disease rather than by habitat loss.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Diallo's argument?$q$, $q$["The frog species lays its eggs in streams, and water quality in the region's streams has declined in areas near cleared forests.","Forests in the region have been cleared for farming at a steady rate for decades, and frogs are now absent from many cleared areas.","Frogs declined as sharply in protected parks as in cleared areas, and dead frogs in both places carried the fungus.","The fungus has been found on frogs in the region, including on frogs of species whose populations have remained stable."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Equal declines in protected and cleared areas, with fungus present in both, point to disease over habitat loss.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Psychologist Marta Ruiz claims that people who write down three things they are grateful for each week become happier over time.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Ruiz's claim?$q$, $q$["Participants who wrote gratitude lists each week reported higher well-being after ten weeks than they had at the start of the study.","Participants who wrote weekly gratitude lists reported higher well-being after ten weeks than participants who wrote weekly lists of neutral events.","Participants who were already happy when the study began were more likely to agree to write weekly gratitude lists.","Participants wrote longer lists in the first week of the study than in the last week."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Comparison with a neutral-writing control shows a specific effect of gratitude. Self-selection of happy people would weaken the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologist Leo Brandt argues that a set of stone circles was used as an astronomical calendar.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Brandt's argument?$q$, $q$["Several stones are aligned with the point on the horizon where the sun rises on the summer and winter solstices.","The stones were transported from a quarry more than twenty kilometers away and arranged in circles of nearly identical diameter.","Pottery and animal bones that may have been used in seasonal feasts were found buried near the center of the circles.","The circles were built over several hundred years, and the oldest stones are arranged differently from the newest ones."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solstice alignments directly support an astronomical function.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$An economist claims that a city's rent control policy reduced the number of rental units available.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the economist's claim?$q$, $q$["Tenants in rent-controlled units moved less often than tenants in other units and reported higher satisfaction with their housing.","Rents for units not covered by the policy rose after it took effect, as did rents in nearby cities without rent control.","The policy was supported by a majority of tenants in the city.","After the policy began, many landlords converted rental buildings into condominiums, and the city's rental units declined."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Conversions of rentals to condos, with a drop in rental units, directly support the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Linguist Akira Sato hypothesizes that a particular sound change in a language spread from older speakers to younger speakers, rather than the reverse.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Sato's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Younger speakers today use the new pronunciation more often than older speakers do, and older speakers report using it less as they age.","The new pronunciation is more common in cities than in rural areas, and city speakers of all ages use it at about the same rate.","Recordings from several decades show the new pronunciation first among older speakers in the 1970s and only later among younger speakers.","Many speakers are unaware that their pronunciation has changed."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Recordings showing the change appearing first among older speakers directly support the direction of spread. Current usage by the young would suggest the opposite.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A team of researchers claims that a new type of roof coating lowers indoor temperatures in summer.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["The coating reflects much more sunlight than standard roofing materials, according to measurements taken on test panels in a laboratory.","In identical buildings, those with the coating had indoor afternoon temperatures several degrees lower than those without it.","Building owners who used the coating reported lower electricity bills in summer, though many also installed new air conditioners.","Building owners who used the coating said they were satisfied with it."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A direct comparison of identical buildings shows lower indoor temperatures.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Historian Sarah Okafor argues that a certain medieval king was more popular among his subjects than later historians have suggested.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Okafor's argument?$q$, $q$["Tax records show that subjects contributed voluntary gifts to the king far more often than they did to his predecessors or successors.","The king commissioned more buildings than any other king of his dynasty, including churches and markets in many of his subjects' towns.","Later historians relied heavily on accounts written by the king's political rivals, who had reason to portray him unfavorably.","The king reigned for more than thirty years, longer than any of his predecessors, and fought several long and costly wars abroad."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Voluntary gifts to the king directly reflect popularity. Reliance on rivals' accounts explains bias but isn't direct evidence of popularity.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Marine scientists claim that a marine reserve, where fishing is banned, has increased the number of fish in nearby fishing areas.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the scientists' claim?$q$, $q$["Fish inside the reserve grew larger and more numerous than fish of the same species in nearby areas where fishing is allowed.","Catches in nearby fishing areas rose after the reserve was established, as did catches in distant areas with no reserve nearby.","Fishers in the region initially opposed the creation of the reserve.","Tagged fish released inside the reserve were often caught in nearby fishing areas, where catches rose after it opened."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Tagged fish moving out and catches rising show spillover benefiting nearby areas.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the essay "Learning to See" by Maya Lindqvist, the author describes how studying painting changed the way she perceives ordinary objects.$q$, $q$Which quotation from "Learning to See" most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"My painting teacher made us redo our sketches several times, insisting that we draw exactly what was in front of us.\"","\"I had always wanted to paint, but I did not take my first lesson until I was forty.\"","\"After a year of drawing lessons, I could no longer look at a coffee cup without noticing the thin crescent of reflected light along its rim.\"","\"The museum near my apartment holds a large collection of still lifes, and I visit it often to study how painters rendered light.\""]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Only the first quotation describes a change in how she perceives ordinary objects.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Neuroscientist Owen Park claims that playing a musical instrument in childhood leads to lasting improvements in the ability to distinguish speech sounds in noisy environments.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Park's claim?$q$, $q$["Children who play instruments are better at understanding speech in noisy classrooms than children who do not play instruments.","Adults who played an instrument only as children outperformed adults who never played on tests of hearing speech in noise.","Adults who currently play an instrument outperform adults who have never played on tests of understanding speech in noise.","Children who play instruments tend to come from families with higher incomes."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Benefits persisting in adults who stopped playing support a lasting effect. Family income is a confound that would weaken the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a certain species of ant farms fungus by deliberately planting fragments of it in prepared chambers.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["When researchers removed the fungus, ants brought fragments from other nests and set them on beds of chewed leaves, where they grew.","Ants of the species are always found near the fungus in the wild, and the fungus is never found growing outside the ants' nests.","When the fungus in a nest became diseased, ants removed the infected portions and carried them out of the nest.","Ants of the species eat the fungus as their main source of food and feed it to their larvae."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ants actively transplanting fungus onto prepared beds directly demonstrates deliberate planting.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian argues that a famous speech was written by the politician who delivered it rather than by a speechwriter.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the historian's argument?$q$, $q$["The speech was praised by newspapers for sounding more personal than the politician's other speeches, which were written by staff.","The politician employed several speechwriters during her career, including during the year in which the speech was delivered.","The speech contained several phrases that the politician often used in conversation and in her letters to friends.","Drafts of the speech in the politician's handwriting, with extensive revisions, were found among her personal papers."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Handwritten drafts with revisions are direct evidence of her authorship. Her employing speechwriters would cut against it.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h94$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Ecologist Lina Bauer hypothesizes that a species of tree produces chemicals in its leaves that discourage deer from eating them, and that the trees produce more of these chemicals after being browsed.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Bauer's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["Deer in the region eat the leaves of many tree species but eat this species' leaves less often than those of most other species.","Trees of the species grow faster in areas without deer, and their leaves there contain about the same levels of the chemicals.","Leaves of browsed trees held more of the chemicals than leaves of unbrowsed trees, and deer avoided them in feeding trials.","The chemicals in the leaves give them a bitter taste, and their levels are similar in browsed and unbrowsed trees."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Higher chemical levels after browsing and avoidance by deer directly support both parts of the hypothesis.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A medical researcher claims that a hospital's new hand-washing program reduced infection rates.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["Infection rates at the hospital fell after the program began, and they fell most in the units where staff had received the training first.","During the same period, the hospital also began using new antibacterial surfaces in patient rooms and reduced the average length of patient stays.","Staff members reported washing their hands more often after the program began, according to an anonymous survey.","Other hospitals in the region adopted similar programs."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Simultaneous changes that could also reduce infections make it unclear whether the program caused the decline.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Musicologist Rosa Ferreira argues that a composer revised her symphony after hearing its first performance, rather than completing it before the premiere.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Ferreira's argument?$q$, $q$["The premiere score differs from the published version in several passages, and letters describe her reworking the finale afterward.","The symphony was performed many times during the composer's lifetime, and audiences at later performances preferred it to the premiere.","The premiere was attended by many leading musicians, several of whom praised the finale in letters written after the concert.","The published version of the symphony is longer than most symphonies of the period, especially in its final movement."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Differences between premiere and published scores plus letters about reworking support post-premiere revision.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h97$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$In the novel The Clockmaker's Apprentice by Jonas Weller, the narrator gradually comes to admire the master he initially resented.$q$, $q$Which quotation from The Clockmaker's Apprentice most effectively illustrates the claim?$q$, $q$["\"The master was strict and spoke little, and in my first months I could not understand why he made me polish the same gear for days.\"","\"I resented the master for his silence and his endless corrections, and I never forgave him.\"","\"The master's clocks were sold in shops as far away as Vienna and Paris, and customers often asked for him by name.\"","\"At first I counted the hours until I could leave; by spring I arrived early, hoping he would show me how he balanced the escapement.\""]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first quotation shows resentment turning into eagerness and admiration. The third denies any change.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h98$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A team of geologists claims that a lake formed when a landslide dammed a river about 2,000 years ago.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the geologists' claim?$q$, $q$["The lake is deepest near its center and shallowest near its outlet, where the river leaves through a narrow, rocky gorge.","The river that feeds the lake has deposited sediment in the valley for millions of years, long before the lake existed.","Radiocarbon dating of trees buried by the landslide and of the oldest sediments in the lake both indicate dates of about 2,000 years ago.","Trees buried by the landslide date to about 2,000 years ago, but the lake's oldest sediments are about 10,000 years old."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Matching dates for the landslide and the lake's oldest sediments directly support the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Economist Carlos Mendes argues that a program giving cash to low-income families improved children's school attendance.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Mendes's argument?$q$, $q$["Families that received the cash spent much of it on school uniforms, books, and transportation to and from school.","Attendance rose in families given the cash but not in similar families randomly assigned to receive none.","School attendance among children in families receiving cash rose after the program began, as it did across the whole region.","Teachers reported that students in families receiving cash seemed more attentive and better prepared in class."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A randomized comparison showing attendance gains only in recipient families supports the claim. A preexisting upward trend would weaken it.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Zoologist Fatima Noor hypothesizes that dolphins use distinctive whistles as names to identify one another.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Noor's hypothesis?$q$, $q$["A dolphin called back when it heard a recording of its own signature whistle but ignored recordings of other dolphins' whistles.","Dolphins in the same group produce nearly identical whistles, which differ noticeably from the whistles produced by other groups.","Dolphins in a group often produce the same whistle at the same time when they are coordinating a hunt.","Dolphins can recognize the whistles of their relatives after being separated from them for many years."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Responding selectively to its own whistle supports the idea that whistles function as names.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A researcher claims that a new teaching method improved students' writing skills.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["Students taught with the new method wrote longer essays, which graders who knew which method each student had used scored higher.","Students taught with the new method said they felt more confident about writing and reported spending more time revising their essays.","Teachers who used the new method rated their students' essays higher than they had rated essays from students in previous years.","Graders who did not know which method students had received gave higher scores to essays written by students taught the new way."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Blind grading showing higher scores is direct evidence of improved writing. Length alone doesn't show quality.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Historian Grace Lin argues that a medieval trade fair attracted merchants from far beyond the surrounding region.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Lin's argument?$q$, $q$["The fair was held every year for several centuries, and records show that merchants traveled to it from many towns in the region.","The town that hosted the fair grew to about five thousand people, many of whom worked as innkeepers and stable hands.","Coins from more than a dozen distant kingdoms, along with goods such as Baltic amber and Persian silk, have been found at the fair's site.","Local farmers sold grain and livestock at the fair to merchants who resold the goods in nearby market towns."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Distant coins and goods directly indicate merchants from far away.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Researchers claim that a certain plant species can sense the sound of flowing water and grow its roots toward it.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers' claim?$q$, $q$["The plant species grows naturally along riverbanks, where its roots spread toward the water's edge.","With no water nearby, potted plants grew roots toward recordings of flowing water but not toward white noise.","Plants grown in pots with moist soil on one side grew roots toward the moist soil even when no sound was played.","Plants grown in pots near speakers playing white noise grew roots in all directions at about the same rate."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Roots growing toward water sounds—but not other noise—without real water present isolate sound as the cue.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Mark Owusu argues that a city's new library branch increased civic participation in the surrounding neighborhood.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Owusu's argument?$q$, $q$["Turnout in the neighborhood rose sharply after the library opened, but it rose by the same amount citywide in that election.","The library hosted dozens of community meetings in its first year, many of them about local elections and city council decisions.","Attendance at neighborhood association meetings rose after the library opened, and many of those meetings were held at the library.","Residents of the neighborhood used the library's computers frequently, including to register to vote."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If turnout rose equally citywide, the library may not explain the neighborhood's increase.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$Paleontologist Ana Ruiz claims that a newly discovered dinosaur species was covered in feathers.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support Ruiz's claim?$q$, $q$["The dinosaur was closely related to several species known to have been feathered, and it shared their lightweight, birdlike bones.","The dinosaur's skeleton was found in fine-grained sediment that often preserves soft tissue such as skin and feathers.","The dinosaur was about the size of a modern turkey.","Impressions of branching, feather-like structures were preserved alongside the fossil's skeleton, especially along its arms and tail."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Direct impressions of feathers are the strongest evidence. Relatedness or preservation conditions are indirect.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian claims that a nineteenth-century newspaper exaggerated the size of a political protest it reported.$q$, $q$Which finding, if true, would most directly support the historian's claim?$q$, $q$["The newspaper strongly supported the protesters' cause and often printed large estimates of turnout at events it favored.","Other newspapers in the city reported the protest but gave no estimate of the size of the crowd.","Police records and photographs of the event show a crowd of about two thousand people, while the newspaper reported more than twenty thousand.","The protest took place in the city's largest public square, which could hold well over twenty thousand people."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Independent records showing a far smaller crowd directly support exaggeration. The paper's sympathies suggest a motive but aren't direct evidence.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying a population of lizards on a Caribbean island measured the lengths of the lizards' front and hind legs before and after a major hurricane. Lizards that survived the storm had, on average, longer front legs and shorter hind legs than the population as a whole had before the hurricane. In wind-tunnel experiments, lizards clinging to perches pulled their hind legs close to their bodies, while their front legs gripped the perch; individuals with long hind legs were more likely to be blown off. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["lizards with long hind legs were better able to survive the hurricane because they could run to shelter more quickly.","the hurricane acted as a selective force, favoring lizards whose body proportions helped them hold on during extreme winds.","the hurricane caused surviving lizards' hind legs to shrink as they clung to perches during the storm.","hind-leg length has no effect on whether a lizard survives an extreme storm."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Survivors had shorter hind legs and longer front legs, and the wind-tunnel results explain why: long hind legs caught the wind. This points to the storm selecting for certain proportions. The storm couldn't have changed individuals' bone lengths, and the evidence contradicts the claims that long hind legs helped or that leg length had no effect.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In many languages, words for colors appear in a predictable order. Languages with only two color terms distinguish dark from light; those with three add a term for red; those with more terms tend to add green or yellow next, then blue. Linguists studying a language with five color terms found that it had words for dark, light, red, green, and yellow. Based on the pattern described, it is reasonable to expect that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the language does not have a separate word for blue.","the language will soon develop a separate word for pink.","speakers of the language cannot perceive the color blue.","the language has a word for blue but not for yellow."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sequence is dark/light, red, green or yellow, then blue. With five terms covering dark, light, red, green, and yellow, the next addition would be blue, so the language likely lacks a blue term. Lacking a word doesn't mean speakers can't perceive the color.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Historians long assumed that a medieval manuscript known as the Codex Varga was copied in a monastery in northern Italy, since its script resembles that used by Italian scribes. A recent analysis, however, found that the parchment was made from the skins of a breed of sheep raised only in the Iberian Peninsula, and that the ink contained a mineral mined only in central Spain at the time. Unless the materials were imported, which was rare for monasteries of the period, the findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["Italian scribes in the medieval period preferred parchment and ink imported from Spain.","the script of the manuscript was copied from an earlier Spanish manuscript rather than an Italian one.","the manuscript was produced in northern Italy using materials that monasteries regularly imported.","the manuscript was more likely produced in Spain, perhaps by a scribe trained in an Italian style of writing."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Both the parchment and ink point to Spain, and importing such materials was rare. That makes Spanish production more likely, which can be reconciled with the Italian-looking script if the scribe was trained in that style. The last choice contradicts the stated rarity of imports.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Economist Farah Siddiqui studied two neighboring towns that both received grants to build public parks. In Town A, the parks were built in the town center; in Town B, they were distributed across several outlying neighborhoods. Five years later, property values had risen substantially in Town A's center but only slightly elsewhere, while in Town B they had risen modestly across many neighborhoods. Siddiqui argues that the location of public investments can influence ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["whether residents choose to use public parks at all in their free time.","the total amount of grant money that towns receive for public projects.","how evenly the economic benefits of those investments are shared across a community.","the total value of all property in a town, since parks built in a town center raise values more than parks built anywhere else."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In Town A, benefits concentrated in the center; in Town B, they spread across neighborhoods. Location thus affected how widely the gains were distributed. The text says nothing about park use, grant amounts, or comparisons with large cities.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Many plants that grow in dry regions open the pores on their leaves, called stomata, only at night, when the air is cooler and more humid. This allows them to take in carbon dioxide while losing less water than they would during the day. However, photosynthesis requires sunlight. To get around this problem, these plants store the carbon dioxide they absorb at night in the form of an acid and then ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["release it into the air during the day through their stomata, which they open briefly at midday to absorb additional sunlight.","release it inside their leaves during the day to be used in photosynthesis while their stomata stay closed.","use it to photosynthesize at night with the help of moonlight and the light of the stars.","convert it directly into water, which they store in their leaves for use during dry periods."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The plants need CO₂ for photosynthesis, which requires daylight, but they keep stomata closed in the day to save water. Storing CO₂ at night and releasing it internally during the day solves both problems. Opening stomata in the day defeats the purpose, and photosynthesis by moonlight contradicts the stated need for sunlight.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In the eighteenth century, sailors suffering from scurvy often recovered after eating citrus fruit. In the following century, however, British naval ships switched from lemons to limes from British colonies in the Caribbean, and outbreaks of scurvy became more common again on long voyages. Since scurvy is caused by a lack of vitamin C, this pattern would be best explained if ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the limes supplied to the ships contained substantially less vitamin C than the lemons they replaced.","sailors on nineteenth-century voyages refused to eat the limes that their ships supplied to them.","nineteenth-century voyages were shorter than the voyages of the eighteenth century.","scurvy in the nineteenth century was caused by a different factor than in the eighteenth century."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If scurvy results from vitamin C deficiency and outbreaks increased after the switch, the likeliest explanation is that limes supplied less vitamin C. Shorter voyages would reduce scurvy, and a different cause contradicts the stated cause.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Some songbirds learn their songs from adult tutors, while others are born knowing their songs. In an experiment, researchers raised young birds of a learning species in isolation, playing them recordings of songs from a different species. As adults, many of the birds sang songs that blended features of their own species' typical song with features of the recordings. This result suggests that young birds of this species ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["are unable to learn any features of songs that they hear from recordings rather than from live tutors.","will learn the song of whatever species they hear during early development, regardless of which species they belong to.","are born knowing their species' complete song and do not need to learn from tutors at all.","are predisposed to learn certain features of their own species' song even without hearing an adult of their species sing."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Isolated birds never heard their own species, yet their songs included their species' typical features alongside recorded ones—indicating an inborn bias toward those features while still learning from what they heard.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study of hospital emergency departments, researchers found that patients treated by physicians at the end of a long shift were prescribed antibiotics more often for conditions that rarely require them, such as colds, than patients treated earlier in the shift. Prescribing an antibiotic, the researchers note, is often the quickest way to satisfy a patient who expects one, whereas explaining why antibiotics won't help takes time and effort. The findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["patients treated later in a shift are more likely to have serious bacterial infections than patients treated earlier.","physicians at the end of long shifts are more knowledgeable about antibiotics than physicians earlier in their shifts.","physician fatigue may lead to decisions that require less effort, even when those decisions are not medically ideal.","antibiotics are the most effective treatment for colds when they are prescribed late in a physician's shift."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Prescribing is easier than explaining, and it increases late in a shift, suggesting that tiredness pushes doctors toward the easier choice. Nothing indicates later patients are sicker.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Dendrochronologists date wooden objects by matching the pattern of thick and thin rings in the wood to a master record of ring patterns from trees of known ages. A wooden beam from a historic barn has an outermost ring corresponding to the year 1782. However, the outer layers of the log, including the bark, were removed when the beam was shaped. Therefore, the tree from which the beam was cut ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["was felled in exactly 1782, the year of its outermost ring.","was felled no earlier than 1782 and possibly some years later.","began growing in 1782 and was cut down many years afterward.","was felled several years before 1782, when the barn was built."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The outermost remaining ring dates to 1782, but outer rings were removed, so the tree grew for some unknown time after 1782. The felling date is therefore 1782 at the earliest.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a series of experiments, participants were asked to choose between receiving $50 today or $55 in one week. Most chose $50 today. When asked to choose between $50 in fifty-two weeks or $55 in fifty-three weeks, most chose the $55. Since both choices involve waiting one additional week for an extra $5, these results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["people weigh a delay more heavily when it begins immediately than when the same delay occurs in the distant future.","people generally prefer larger rewards to smaller ones regardless of how long they must wait to receive them.","participants misunderstood the second question and believed they would receive $55 sooner than $50.","most participants valued the extra $5 more highly in the first choice than in the second choice."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The trade-off is identical (one week for $5), yet choices flip depending on whether the delay starts now. That means immediate delays feel costlier than distant ones. The first choice contradicts most people choosing $50 now.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Many deep-sea fish produce their own light through bioluminescence. Biologists have noticed that in several species, light-producing organs called photophores are located on the fish's underside and emit a dim blue glow. In the dim ocean depths, where faint sunlight filters down from above, a fish's silhouette can be visible to predators looking up from below. The location and color of these photophores suggest that they help fish ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["attract prey from the darker water below by producing a bright signal that is visible from great distances in the ocean depths.","see prey swimming above them by illuminating the water over their heads.","communicate with other fish of their species through patterns of flashing light.","blend in with the faint light from above, making their silhouettes harder for predators below to see."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Photophores on the underside emitting dim blue light would match faint downwelling light, hiding the fish's silhouette from predators below. The light is dim and directed downward, not a bright long-distance signal.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A city introduced a program offering free public transit to residents over age sixty-five. In the program's first year, ridership among older residents rose by 30 percent. However, surveys showed that most of the new trips were made by people who previously had not traveled at all, rather than by people who had previously driven. City planners had hoped the program would reduce traffic congestion. These results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the program failed to attract older residents who had not previously used public transit.","most older residents in the city drove more after the program began than they had before.","the program increased older residents' mobility but did little to reduce the number of cars on the road.","traffic congestion in the city increased because older residents switched from transit to driving."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Ridership rose mostly from people who hadn't traveled before, not former drivers, so mobility increased while car traffic likely didn't fall much.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologists excavating an ancient settlement found that the oldest layers contained bones of wild deer and gazelle, while later layers contained mostly bones of sheep and goats. Notably, the sheep and goat bones in the later layers came overwhelmingly from young males, while females were found mostly at older ages. Since herders who raise animals for meat and milk typically slaughter most young males and keep females for breeding and milking, the findings suggest that the residents ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["hunted wild sheep and goats more often than they hunted deer and gazelle.","shifted from hunting wild animals to herding domesticated ones.","kept male sheep and goats for breeding and slaughtered most females when young.","abandoned the settlement after the population of wild deer declined."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The shift from wild game to sheep and goats, combined with a culling pattern typical of herders, indicates a move from hunting to herding. The bones show the opposite of keeping males for breeding.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers investigating why some children are picky eaters found that children who were repeatedly offered a disliked vegetable—without being pressured to eat it—gradually began eating more of it, often after eight to fifteen exposures. Children who were pressured or rewarded for eating the vegetable showed less improvement. The findings suggest that parents hoping to expand children's diets should ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["keep offering disliked foods patiently rather than pushing children to eat them.","stop offering foods that children have refused more than once or twice.","reward children whenever they eat a disliked food, since rewards make children more willing to try that food again later.","offer children only foods that they already enjoy until they are older."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Repeated, pressure-free exposure worked better than pressure or rewards, supporting patient repeated offering.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Astronomers can estimate the age of a star cluster by examining its brightest stars. Massive stars burn through their fuel faster than smaller stars and leave the main stage of their lives sooner. In a young cluster, the most massive stars are still shining; in an older cluster, they have already burned out, leaving only smaller stars. If one cluster contains many very massive, bright blue stars and another contains only small, dimmer stars, the first cluster is most likely ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["older than the second cluster.","about the same age as the second cluster.","farther from Earth than the second cluster.","younger than the second cluster."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Massive stars burn out first, so a cluster still containing them is younger than one in which only smaller stars remain.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In medieval Europe, many towns held weekly markets on a fixed day. Historian Lukas Brandt found that neighboring towns rarely held their markets on the same day of the week; instead, market days were usually staggered so that each town in a region held its market on a different day. Brandt argues that this arrangement benefited traveling merchants, who ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["preferred to sell their goods in a single town each week rather than travel between several different towns.","were required by law to attend the market in their own town on the day it was held.","could visit several markets in a single week without having to choose between towns holding markets at the same time.","competed directly with one another by setting up stalls in neighboring towns on the same day."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Staggered market days let a merchant move from town to town across the week. The other choices don't explain why staggering would benefit traveling merchants.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$When the zebra mussel, an invasive species, spread through the Great Lakes, it filtered huge quantities of plankton from the water. The water became much clearer. Surprisingly, populations of some underwater plants increased sharply, even though the mussels did not eat or otherwise directly affect those plants. This is likely because ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the mussels released nutrients that the underwater plants needed to grow.","clearer water allowed more sunlight to reach plants growing on the lake bottom.","plankton had previously been eating the underwater plants in large quantities.","the underwater plants fed on the zebra mussels after the mussels died."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The mussels' only stated effect was clearer water; more light reaching the bottom would boost plant growth. The text says the mussels didn't directly affect the plants, ruling out the nutrient explanation.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A 2019 survey found that 70 percent of adults in a certain country said they would be willing to pay more for products made with recycled materials. However, sales data from the same year showed that products labeled as made from recycled materials accounted for less than 10 percent of purchases in categories where such products were available at only slightly higher prices. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["people's stated preferences in surveys may not reliably predict their purchasing behavior.","most adults in the country were unaware that recycled products were available to purchase.","products made with recycled materials were much more expensive than other products.","the survey was conducted among a group of people who did not buy any products."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Many said they'd pay more, yet few bought recycled products even when the price difference was small—so stated preferences didn't match behavior. The text says prices were only slightly higher.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Linguists have observed that when two languages come into contact, speakers often borrow nouns from each other readily, while verbs and grammatical structures are borrowed far less often. In one region where two languages have been spoken side by side for centuries, researchers found that each language had borrowed hundreds of nouns from the other but only a handful of verbs. This pattern is consistent with the idea that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["speakers of the two languages borrowed nouns mainly because neither language originally had words for the objects involved.","nouns are generally more difficult to learn than verbs in a second language.","the two languages will soon merge into a single language with a shared grammar.","the grammar of a language tends to be more resistant to outside influence than its vocabulary for things."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Many nouns but few verbs and grammatical structures were borrowed, consistent with grammar resisting borrowing more than vocabulary for things.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers found that bees exposed to a common fungicide were less able to fight off a particular parasite than unexposed bees. The fungicide itself did not harm bees in the absence of the parasite. However, in regions where the fungicide was widely used, beekeepers reported higher colony losses only in years when the parasite was common. Together, these findings suggest that the fungicide ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["is a more important direct cause of colony losses than the parasite is in regions where the fungicide is widely used.","protects bees from the parasite in years when the parasite is especially common.","may harm bee colonies mainly by weakening their defenses against the parasite rather than directly.","has no effect on bee colonies regardless of whether the parasite is present."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The fungicide caused no harm alone but reduced resistance to the parasite, and losses rose only in parasite years—an indirect effect through weakened defenses.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$During the eighteenth century, the price of a book in England was so high that many readers could not afford to buy one. Circulating libraries allowed readers to borrow books for a small fee. Records show that the most frequently borrowed books at these libraries were novels, while sermons and histories, which made up a large share of books sold, were borrowed less often. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["novels were more expensive to buy than sermons and histories, which is why readers were more likely to borrow them from libraries.","the books people chose to borrow may differ from those that sold well, perhaps because buyers and borrowers differed.","circulating libraries refused to lend sermons and histories to readers who paid a fee.","most readers in eighteenth-century England preferred owning books to borrowing them."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Borrowing and buying patterns differed, suggesting that the two groups—those able to buy and those who borrowed—had different tastes or needs. Nothing indicates libraries refused to lend some books.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A study of commuters found that those who biked to work reported higher job satisfaction than those who drove. But the researchers also found that the bikers, on average, lived closer to their workplaces and had more flexible schedules. Before concluding that biking itself increases job satisfaction, the researchers would need to ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["account for the possibility that shorter commutes and flexible schedules, rather than biking, explain the difference.","survey commuters who drove to work about whether they enjoyed driving.","find out whether commuters who biked to work had been more satisfied with their jobs before they began biking than afterward.","show that commuters who drove to work lived farther from their workplaces than those who biked."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Commute distance and schedule flexibility differ between groups and could explain satisfaction; they must be ruled out before attributing the effect to biking.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Scientists have long wondered how the ancient Polynesians, who had no written maps, navigated across thousands of kilometers of open ocean. Studies of traditional navigators who still practice the old methods show that they memorize the rising and setting points of dozens of stars, read the patterns of ocean swells reflected off distant islands, and watch for birds that fly out to sea in the morning and return to land at night. This evidence suggests that Polynesian navigation relied on ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["instruments similar to compasses that allowed navigators to determine direction precisely.","luck and chance discoveries rather than any systematic method of finding land.","maps carved into wood or stone that were carried on long voyages.","detailed knowledge of natural signs that could be learned and passed on without written records."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Navigators used memorized star positions, swell patterns, and bird behavior—natural signs requiring no written maps or instruments.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In the 1950s, a dam was built on a river that had flooded nearby farmland nearly every spring. The flooding ended, and farmers initially welcomed the change. Within two decades, however, crop yields on the farmland had declined, and farmers had to buy increasing amounts of fertilizer. The decline likely occurred because the floods had ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["washed away the fertilizer that farmers had applied to their fields, forcing them to buy more fertilizer every spring.","prevented farmers from planting crops until late in the growing season.","deposited nutrient-rich sediment on the fields each year, which stopped once the dam was built.","carried pests away from the fields that later damaged the farmers' crops."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After the floods stopped, yields fell and farmers needed more fertilizer, implying the floods had been supplying nutrients through sediment.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers placed identical bird feeders in two parts of a park. One area was close to a busy road; the other was in a quiet woodland. Birds at the roadside feeders spent more time looking around and less time eating than birds at the woodland feeders, and they fled sooner when a researcher approached. The researchers suggested that traffic noise may ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["attract more birds to the roadside feeders than to the feeders in the woodland.","make it harder for birds to hear approaching predators, causing them to compensate with greater visual vigilance.","make birds at the roadside feeders less wary of humans, since they have grown used to the noise and movement of traffic.","cause birds at the roadside feeders to eat more food than birds at the woodland feeders."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Roadside birds watched more and fled sooner—behavior consistent with relying on sight because noise masks sounds of predators. They were more, not less, fearful.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Some economists argue that raising the price of a product will always reduce the quantity people buy. Yet for certain luxury goods, such as designer handbags, sales sometimes rise after prices increase. One explanation is that, for these goods, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["a high price itself can make an item more desirable by signaling exclusivity or status.","manufacturers produce fewer items when prices rise, making each item more durable.","most buyers are unaware of the prices of the items they purchase.","the materials used to make the items become cheaper when prices rise."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$If higher prices increase sales, the price itself must add appeal, as with status goods. The other choices don't explain increased demand.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Mammals that hibernate dramatically lower their body temperatures and heart rates during winter, surviving for months on stored fat. Researchers studying a species of ground squirrel found that individuals periodically warm up to normal body temperature for about a day during hibernation before cooling down again, even though these arousals use a large share of their stored energy. Since the arousals are costly, they likely ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["occur because the squirrels have used up all of their stored fat.","happen only in squirrels that failed to store enough fat before winter.","allow the squirrels to save energy compared with remaining cold throughout the winter.","serve some essential function that cannot be performed at low body temperatures."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$If arousals consume much energy yet still occur, they must provide some necessary benefit unavailable at low temperatures. They cost energy, so they don't save it.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study of reading comprehension, students read a passage either on paper or on a screen. Both groups scored similarly on questions about specific details. However, students who read on paper scored higher on questions requiring them to recall the order of events in the passage. The researchers suggested that the physical experience of turning pages may help readers ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["remember specific details more accurately than readers who use screens.","read more quickly than readers who use screens for the same passage.","build a mental map of a text's structure, which supports memory of sequence.","avoid distractions from notifications and advertisements on screens."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Paper readers were better only at recalling order, which suggests that physical page-turning supports a sense of the text's structure. Detail recall was similar.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The town's historical society holds the diaries of two sisters who lived in the town during the 1918 influenza pandemic. One sister, a nurse, describes overcrowded hospitals and dozens of deaths each week. The other, who lived on a farm outside town, mentions the epidemic only once, noting that a neighbor had fallen ill. A historian relying only on the second sister's diary might ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["conclude that the pandemic began in the countryside and spread to the town.","underestimate how severely the pandemic affected the town.","assume that both sisters worked as nurses during the pandemic.","overestimate the number of deaths in the town during the pandemic."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The farm sister barely mentions the epidemic, so relying on her account alone would understate its severity in town.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers trained dogs to sit in an MRI scanner while they were shown either their owners or strangers giving a hand signal associated with a food reward. The dogs' brain regions associated with reward were more active when the signal came from their owners. Because the reward was identical in both cases, this result suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the dogs found something rewarding about their owners beyond the food itself.","the dogs could not tell the difference between their owners and strangers.","the dogs expected to receive more food from their owners than from strangers.","the dogs were trained more thoroughly by their owners than by the researchers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since the food reward was the same, the greater reward-region activity for owners suggests the owners themselves were rewarding.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In many cities, buildings painted white or covered with reflective materials stay cooler in summer than darker buildings. But in a study of a city in a cold climate, researchers found that white roofs increased total annual energy use for some buildings. This would be expected if ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the buildings with white roofs used air conditioning more often in summer.","darker buildings reflected more sunlight than white buildings in winter.","the city's summers were longer and hotter than its winters.","the extra heating needed in winter outweighed the cooling saved in summer."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$White roofs save cooling energy but reflect heat that would help in winter. In a cold climate, the added heating could outweigh the summer savings.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying a group of mountain villages found that villages located farther from roads had more distinct local dialects than villages close to roads. Older residents in the remote villages also reported that young people who moved to cities often lost their village dialect within a few years. Taken together, these observations suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["villages close to roads were founded more recently than the remote villages.","young people in remote villages speak their village dialect more than older residents do.","regular contact with outsiders tends to reduce differences between local ways of speaking.","dialects in remote villages change more rapidly than dialects in villages near roads."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Isolation correlates with distinct dialects, and moving to cities erodes them—both suggest that contact with outsiders makes speech more uniform.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Some trees in the forests of eastern North America produce large crops of nuts only every few years, with very small crops in between. Ecologists propose that this pattern helps the trees reproduce: in years with small crops, populations of nut-eating animals decline, so in the next large-crop year ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["animals have more nuts available than they need and stop eating them altogether.","there are too few animals to eat all the nuts, and more of them survive to sprout.","the trees produce nuts that are too small for animals to eat easily.","animal populations increase so quickly that they eat every nut the trees produce."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Small crops shrink animal populations; then a large crop overwhelms the remaining animals, leaving more nuts to germinate.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A researcher studied a set of letters written by a nineteenth-century merchant to his business partner. In letters written before 1850, the merchant frequently mentioned goods shipped by sailing vessel and complained about delays caused by weather. After 1850, he mentioned steamships more often, and complaints about weather nearly disappeared, though he began to mention the rising cost of coal. These changes suggest that the shift to steamships ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["made shipping less dependent on weather while introducing a new kind of expense.","made shipping more expensive than it had been before 1850 in every respect.","caused the merchant to stop doing business with his partner after 1850.","did not change how the merchant's goods were transported."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Weather complaints faded while coal costs appeared—steam reduced weather dependence but added fuel expense.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In an experiment, researchers gave two groups of people the same plain cookies. One group was told the cookies were made with expensive organic ingredients; the other was given no information. The first group rated the cookies as tastier and said they would pay more for them. This finding suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["organic ingredients make cookies taste better than conventional ingredients do.","people who buy organic food are more likely to enjoy cookies than other people are.","the two groups were given cookies that were made from different ingredients.","people's judgments of how food tastes can be influenced by information about the food."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The cookies were identical, so the difference in ratings must come from the information given, not the ingredients.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$When a forest is cleared for farming, the soil often loses much of its organic carbon within a few decades. Researchers studying abandoned farmland that had been allowed to regrow into forest found that soil carbon recovered, but slowly—after eighty years, levels were still well below those in forests that had never been cleared. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["farmland that is abandoned quickly loses even more soil carbon than active farmland.","soil carbon in regrown forests will eventually exceed that in uncleared forests, since the regrowing trees absorb carbon quickly.","protecting existing forests may preserve soil carbon more effectively than relying on regrowth after clearing.","clearing forests for farming has no lasting effect on the carbon stored in soil."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Carbon is lost quickly but recovers slowly, so preventing loss is more effective than relying on regrowth to restore it.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The novel's narrator repeatedly insists that she has forgiven her brother for selling the family farm. Yet she describes the new owners' changes to the property in bitter detail, refuses to attend her brother's birthday dinner, and keeps the deed she was supposed to have handed over. These details suggest that the narrator ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["is unaware that her brother sold the family farm to new owners.","has not truly forgiven her brother, despite what she claims.","approves of the changes the new owners have made to the property.","has forgiven her brother and wants to repair their relationship."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Her actions—bitterness, avoidance, keeping the deed—contradict her claim of forgiveness.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Studies of early human settlements in a river valley show that the earliest farmers grew a single variety of wheat. Over the next thousand years, the number of wheat varieties grown in the valley increased to more than a dozen, each suited to a different soil type or growing season. Over the same period, the valley's population grew substantially. One possible explanation linking these developments is that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["growing varieties suited to different conditions allowed farmers to cultivate more land and feed more people.","a larger population forced farmers to rely on a single high-yielding variety of wheat that could be grown in every soil type.","farmers stopped growing wheat altogether as the valley's population increased.","the earliest farmers in the valley grew more wheat than later farmers did."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Varieties adapted to diverse soils and seasons would let farmers use more land, supporting a larger population.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A team of researchers measured how quickly glaciers in a mountain range were shrinking. Glaciers on north-facing slopes, which receive less direct sunlight, were shrinking more slowly than those on south-facing slopes. However, a few north-facing glaciers were shrinking just as quickly as south-facing ones; these were all covered with a layer of dark dust blown from a nearby desert. This observation suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["north-facing glaciers shrink more slowly because the cold desert winds that carry dust keep them cooler than south-facing ones.","dust from the desert slows the melting of glaciers on south-facing slopes.","the direction a glacier faces has no effect on how quickly it shrinks.","dark dust may speed melting by causing glaciers to absorb more sunlight, offsetting the advantage of a shaded slope."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Dust-covered north-facing glaciers melted as fast as sunlit ones, suggesting dark dust increased heat absorption and canceled the shading advantage.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Paleontologists have found fossilized dinosaur eggs arranged in tight circular clutches, with the narrow ends of the eggs pointing toward the center. In modern birds, this arrangement is associated with species in which a parent sits on the eggs to keep them warm, since it allows the parent's body to cover them evenly. Crocodiles, which bury their eggs and leave them, lay eggs in loose, irregular piles. The arrangement of the fossil eggs therefore suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the dinosaurs that laid them buried their eggs and left them, as crocodiles do with their own eggs.","the eggs were moved into circular arrangements by water or sediment after they had been laid.","the dinosaurs that laid them may have incubated their eggs by sitting on them, as many birds do.","the dinosaurs that laid them were more closely related to crocodiles than to modern birds."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The tight circular clutch matches the pattern of birds that sit on eggs, unlike crocodiles' loose piles, suggesting similar brooding behavior.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In an effort to reduce the number of people who skipped medical appointments, a clinic began sending text reminders two days before each appointment. Missed appointments fell by 15 percent. The following year, the clinic changed the wording of the reminders to mention that skipping an appointment meant another patient could not be seen in that slot. Missed appointments fell by an additional 20 percent. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["patients are more likely to attend appointments when reminders are sent two days in advance rather than one.","reminding patients of how their absence affects others can be more effective than a simple reminder alone.","the text reminders became less effective in the second year than they had been in the first year.","most patients who skipped appointments did so because they had forgotten when their appointments were."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Adding a message about effects on others produced a further decline beyond the plain reminder.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying a remote island found that its population of a small flightless bird had declined sharply after rats arrived on supply ships. Once the rats were eradicated, the bird population recovered within a decade. On a neighboring island where rats had never arrived, the bird population remained stable throughout the same period. Together, these observations most strongly suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the rats were responsible for the decline of the bird population on the first island.","the bird population on the neighboring island would have declined without the eradication program.","the birds on the first island recovered because they migrated from the neighboring island.","supply ships brought the birds to the first island along with the rats."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The decline followed the rats' arrival and reversed after their removal, while the rat-free island stayed stable—pointing to the rats as the cause.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Art historians have long debated whether the painter Jan Verhaegen created a particular portrait himself or whether it was painted by one of his students. Infrared imaging recently revealed a detailed underdrawing beneath the paint, with many corrections and changes in the position of the figure's hands. Since Verhaegen's students typically copied finished compositions without making changes, while Verhaegen himself often revised his designs as he worked, the underdrawing suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the portrait is a copy of an earlier painting that Verhaegen had already finished.","one of Verhaegen's students corrected mistakes that Verhaegen had made in the portrait.","Verhaegen's students made more corrections to their paintings than Verhaegen did.","the portrait is more likely to have been painted by Verhaegen than by one of his students."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Revisions in the underdrawing match Verhaegen's habit of revising, unlike students who copied without changes.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$When researchers analyzed the remains of meals at an ancient coastal settlement, they found that shellfish made up most of the diet in the earliest layers. In later layers, the shells were noticeably smaller on average, and species that live in deeper water became more common. These changes are consistent with the possibility that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["residents had developed a preference for smaller shellfish because they found the larger ones less flavorful.","the deeper-water species had grown larger over time, replacing the shallow-water species in the diet.","residents had overharvested the easily reached shellfish, forcing them to gather smaller ones and to seek food farther from shore.","the settlement's population had grown so large that residents began to prefer deeper-water species, which they could catch in greater numbers."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Shrinking shell size and a shift to deeper species fit a pattern of depleting nearby stocks. A smaller population would ease pressure, not cause these changes.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Some economists have argued that automation will eliminate many jobs. Economist Lars Eriksson notes, however, that when automated teller machines became common in the 1980s and 1990s, the number of bank tellers in the United States actually increased. ATMs reduced the number of tellers needed per branch, which lowered the cost of operating a branch, and banks responded by opening many more branches. This example suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["automated teller machines were less reliable than human tellers, so banks hired additional tellers at every branch to serve as backups.","automation can reduce the labor needed for a task while increasing demand for workers by making related activities cheaper.","the number of tellers needed per branch increased after banks began to install automated teller machines.","banks opened more branches in the 1980s and 1990s for reasons unrelated to the cost of operating them."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$ATMs cut tellers per branch but made branches cheaper, so banks opened more, raising total employment.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Historians studying the diaries of soldiers in a nineteenth-century war noticed that entries written in the first months of the war frequently described battles as glorious adventures. Entries written by the same soldiers a year later rarely used such language and focused instead on hunger, illness, and the deaths of friends. This shift suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the soldiers' direct experience of war changed how they understood and described it.","the soldiers who wrote the later entries were different people from those who wrote the earlier ones.","the war became more glorious as it continued and the soldiers grew more confident.","the soldiers were ordered by their officers to stop describing battles in their diaries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The same soldiers' language changed from glory to hardship after a year, suggesting experience reshaped their view.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In an experiment on decision-making, participants chose among jams at a grocery store display. When the display offered six flavors, about 30 percent of people who stopped bought a jar. When it offered twenty-four flavors, more people stopped to look, but only about 3 percent bought a jar. These results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["people are more likely to buy a product when a store offers a wider variety of options.","most shoppers dislike jam and are unlikely to buy it regardless of how many flavors are offered.","the larger display attracted fewer shoppers than the smaller one did.","offering more choices can attract interest while making people less likely to decide."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$More people stopped at the larger display but far fewer bought, so variety drew interest yet discouraged choosing.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Scientists studying a population of wild chimpanzees found that individuals who spent more time grooming others had more allies who supported them in conflicts. When the researchers tracked the chimpanzees over several years, they found that those who groomed more also tended to rise in the group's social hierarchy. These observations suggest that, for chimpanzees, grooming may ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["be a sign of low rank within the group's social hierarchy.","reduce the number of conflicts that occur within the group.","help build relationships that provide social advantages.","be performed mainly to remove parasites from other chimpanzees."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Grooming correlated with more allies and rising rank, suggesting it builds useful relationships.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A small river town experienced severe flooding roughly once every ten years throughout the nineteenth century. In the 1920s, upstream forests were cleared for farming, and the town began flooding nearly every three years. Since forests absorb rainfall and slow the flow of water into rivers, the change in flood frequency was likely caused by ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["a decrease in the amount of rain that fell in the region after the 1920s.","faster runoff of rainwater into the river after the upstream forests were removed.","the town's construction of new buildings closer to the river in the nineteenth century.","the upstream farms absorbing more rainwater than the forests had absorbed before."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Forests slow runoff; removing them would speed water into the river, raising flood frequency.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h156$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study of bilingual speakers of Spanish and English, participants described a video of a person accidentally knocking over a vase. When describing the event in English, participants usually said "She broke the vase." When describing it in Spanish, they more often used a construction meaning "The vase broke." Later, participants were asked to remember who had caused various accidents. Those who had described events in Spanish were less likely to remember who caused them. The findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the way a language describes events may influence what speakers remember about them.","Spanish speakers are generally less observant than English speakers when watching videos.","participants remembered accidents better when they described them in Spanish than in English.","English and Spanish describe accidental events using the same grammatical construction."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The grammatical framing (agent vs. no agent) corresponded with what speakers later remembered about who caused events.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h157$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A company tested two versions of its website. In one, the price of a product was shown as $99; in the other, it was shown as $100. The $99 version produced substantially more sales, even though the difference in price was only one dollar. A behavioral economist suggested that shoppers ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["preferred the $100 version because they believed higher prices indicated higher quality.","were unable to see the price on the version of the website showing $100.","calculated the exact difference in price and decided that one dollar was a significant saving.","may focus heavily on the first digit of a price, perceiving $99 as much cheaper than $100."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A one-dollar difference wouldn't usually matter much, so the large effect suggests shoppers perceive $99 as substantially lower because of its leading digit.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h158$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying monarch butterflies raised one group of caterpillars on milkweed plants with high levels of a toxin and another group on milkweed with low levels. As adults, butterflies from the first group were rarely eaten by birds in feeding experiments, while those from the second group were eaten more often. Since monarchs store the toxin from milkweed in their bodies, these results suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["birds prefer to eat monarch butterflies that have high levels of toxin in their bodies.","monarch caterpillars that eat high-toxin milkweed grow into larger adult butterflies that birds find more difficult to capture.","the protection monarchs have against predators depends partly on the plants they ate as caterpillars.","the toxin in milkweed has no effect on whether birds eat monarch butterflies."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Butterflies from high-toxin plants were avoided by birds, so protection depends on the caterpillars' food.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h159$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$For centuries, the canals of Venice were cleaned by periodically draining sections and removing accumulated mud by hand. When this practice was largely abandoned in the twentieth century, residents began noticing that the water in many canals had become shallower and that some buildings' foundations were exposed to more frequent flooding at high tide. These developments suggest that the cleaning practice had ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["caused the flooding of building foundations by removing too much mud from the canals.","helped maintain the depth of the canals, which the city's buildings and drainage depended on.","been abandoned because the canals no longer accumulated mud in the twentieth century.","made the canals shallower over time by disturbing the mud at the bottom of the water."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After cleaning stopped, canals grew shallower and flooding increased, suggesting cleaning had maintained depth.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h160$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study of workplace productivity, employees who worked from home one day a week were compared with employees who worked in the office every day. The home-workers completed slightly more tasks, but the researchers noted that the home-workers had volunteered for the arrangement, while the office workers had not been given a choice. Because of this difference, ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the study cannot determine whether working from home itself caused the difference in productivity.","the study shows that working from home reduces productivity for most employees.","the office workers must have been more productive than the home-workers in the study.","the home-workers would probably have completed even more tasks if they had been allowed to work from home every day of the week."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Volunteers may differ from non-volunteers (e.g., more motivated), so the study can't isolate the effect of working from home.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h161$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Fossil pollen from lake sediments shows that, around 4,000 years ago, the plant life near a particular lake changed from mostly oak trees to mostly grasses. Charcoal in the same layers increased sharply at the time of the change, and archaeological sites nearby show that farming communities arrived shortly before. Together, these findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["a natural decline in oak trees caused farming communities to move to the area.","grasses near the lake were burned by early farmers to make room for oak trees.","the climate near the lake became wetter, allowing grasses to replace oak trees.","early farmers may have used fire to clear forests around the lake for agriculture."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Farmers arrived, charcoal increased, and oaks gave way to grasses—consistent with burning forests to clear land.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h162$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a laboratory study, people who were told that a painkiller was expensive reported greater pain relief than people told that the same pill was cheap. In fact, both groups received a pill with no active ingredient. This result suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["expensive painkillers contain more active ingredients than inexpensive ones do.","people who pay more for medicine are less likely to experience pain.","expectations about a treatment can influence how effective people perceive it to be.","the participants could tell that the pills contained no active ingredient."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Both groups received inactive pills, so the difference reflects expectations created by price.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h163$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Studies of ancient Egyptian mummies have revealed high rates of hardened arteries, a condition often associated with modern diets and sedentary lifestyles. However, most of the mummies studied were of wealthy individuals, who likely ate diets rich in meat and fat and did little physical labor. The findings therefore suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["ancient Egyptians of all social classes had higher rates of hardened arteries than people today.","the condition may be linked to diet and activity levels rather than being unique to modern life.","hardened arteries were caused mainly by the process of mummification itself.","poor ancient Egyptians had higher rates of hardened arteries than wealthy Egyptians."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Wealthy Egyptians with rich diets and little labor had the condition, supporting a link to diet and activity rather than modernity. The sample doesn't tell us about all classes.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h164$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$When the city of Seoul removed an elevated highway in 2003 and restored the stream that had been buried beneath it, traffic engineers predicted severe congestion. Instead, traffic in the surrounding area changed little. Researchers found that many drivers had switched to public transit or changed their routes, and some had stopped making certain trips altogether. This outcome suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["traffic levels may adjust to the capacity of roads, rather than remaining fixed.","the elevated highway had carried very few cars before it was removed.","public transit in Seoul became more expensive after the highway was removed.","traffic engineers accurately predicted the effects of removing the highway."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Drivers adapted by changing modes, routes, or trips, so traffic fell to match reduced capacity rather than overwhelming streets.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h165$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers found that people who were shown a list of words including "bed," "rest," "tired," and "dream" often later "remembered" seeing the word "sleep," even though it was never on the list. This suggests that memory ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["records every word a person sees with complete accuracy.","tends to remember words with similar meanings less accurately than words with unrelated meanings.","works better for long lists of words than for short lists.","may reconstruct information based on associations rather than recording it exactly."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$People recalled a related word that wasn't shown, indicating memory fills in from associations.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h166$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Linguists studying a language spoken by about two thousand people found that the language has more than a dozen words for different types of snow but only one word for "car." The community lives in a mountainous region and relies on sled travel in winter; cars became common there only in the last few decades. This pattern suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the language will soon replace its words for snow with words for different types of cars.","speakers of the language are unable to distinguish among different types of cars.","a language's vocabulary tends to reflect distinctions that matter in its speakers' daily lives.","the community has always relied more on cars than on sleds for travel in winter."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Detailed snow vocabulary in a sled-dependent community, and little car vocabulary for a recent technology, suggest vocabulary tracks what matters locally.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h167$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Many coastal towns built seawalls to protect beaches from erosion. Geologists later observed that beaches in front of seawalls often became narrower over time, while beaches without seawalls in the same areas remained stable. Waves striking a wall, they noted, reflect back toward the sea, carrying sand with them. These observations suggest that seawalls ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["prevent the loss of sand from beaches by stopping waves before they reach the shore.","can protect buildings behind them while accelerating the loss of sand from the beaches in front of them.","have no effect on beaches because waves reflect back toward the sea in all locations.","cause beaches on either side of the walls, where there are no seawalls, to become narrower over time as well."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Reflected waves carry sand away, so beaches in front of walls narrow, while unwalled beaches remain stable.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h168$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A study tracked people who won large lottery prizes. A year later, their reported happiness was only slightly higher than before their win, and some reported enjoying everyday pleasures less than they had previously. The researchers suggested that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["people may adapt to major positive changes, so their effect on happiness fades over time.","winning a lottery prize reduces people's happiness over time, since most winners become less satisfied with their lives.","lottery winners were unhappier than most people before they won their prizes.","everyday pleasures become more enjoyable after a person wins a large prize."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Happiness returned near baseline and everyday pleasures dimmed, consistent with adaptation.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h169$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers placed cameras near water holes in an African savanna. They found that zebras and wildebeest visited the water holes mostly during the middle of the day, when lions—which hunt mainly at night and at dawn—were resting. However, during the driest months, when many water holes dried up, zebras and wildebeest visited the remaining water holes at all hours. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["lions changed their hunting habits during the driest months, hunting at midday when zebras and wildebeest gathered at water holes.","zebras and wildebeest prefer to drink at night when lions are most active.","the number of lions near the water holes increased during the driest months.","the animals' need for water during droughts may outweigh the risk of encountering predators."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Normally the animals avoid lion hours, but in drought they come at all hours, suggesting thirst overrides predator avoidance.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h170$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a study, researchers gave one group of students a difficult puzzle and told them it measured intelligence. Another group received the same puzzle but was told it was a game. Students in the "game" group spent more time on the puzzle and solved it more often. This finding suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the students in the \"intelligence\" group were less intelligent than the other students.","games are generally more difficult to solve than tests of intelligence.","framing a task as a test of ability can make people less willing to persist at it.","telling students that a task measures intelligence makes them more motivated to succeed."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$With the same puzzle, the "intelligence" framing reduced persistence and success, while the "game" framing increased them.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h171$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Ecologists found that a species of wildflower produced fewer seeds in areas where bumblebee populations had declined, even when the flowers received plenty of visits from other insects such as flies. The ecologists noted that the flower's pollen is released only when it is vibrated at a particular frequency, which bumblebees produce by buzzing their flight muscles. These findings suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["flies are more effective pollinators of the wildflower than bumblebees are.","the wildflower depends on bumblebees in particular for effective pollination.","the wildflower produces more seeds when it receives visits from many kinds of insects.","bumblebee populations declined because the wildflower produced fewer seeds."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Seed production fell with bumblebees despite other visitors, and only bumblebees' buzzing releases the pollen—so the flower relies on them.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h172$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Before the invention of refrigeration, ice harvested from frozen lakes in New England was shipped as far as India and the Caribbean. Much of the ice melted during the voyage, yet the trade remained profitable for decades. This suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the ice that survived the journey was valuable enough to make up for what was lost.","ships carrying ice traveled much faster than other ships of the period.","ice in India and the Caribbean was cheaper than ice from New England.","very little of the ice melted during the long voyages, since ships carrying ice were insulated well enough to prevent most melting."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Despite heavy melting, the trade stayed profitable, so the remaining ice must have commanded high prices.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h173$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Ornithologists noticed that city-dwelling great tits, a type of songbird, sing at a higher pitch than great tits living in forests. Urban noise from traffic and machinery is concentrated at low frequencies. Researchers suggested that urban great tits ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["sing at higher pitches because they are physically smaller than great tits in forests.","are unable to hear low-pitched songs because of the noise in cities.","sing at higher pitches to imitate the sounds of traffic and machinery.","may sing at higher pitches so that their songs can be heard above low-frequency city noise."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$City noise is low-pitched, so singing higher avoids being masked.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h174$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers asked people to estimate the number of deaths caused each year by various hazards. Participants greatly overestimated deaths from dramatic, widely reported events such as shark attacks and plane crashes, while underestimating deaths from common causes such as diabetes and falls. This pattern suggests that people's estimates of risk ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["are generally accurate for common causes of death but not for rare ones.","are based mainly on careful study of official statistics about causes of death.","may be shaped by how easily examples come to mind rather than by actual frequency.","tend to underestimate the dangers of dramatic and widely reported events."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Vivid, widely reported events were overestimated and mundane causes underestimated, consistent with relying on ease of recall.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h175$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In 1883, the eruption of Krakatoa in Indonesia released enormous quantities of ash and gas into the upper atmosphere. For several years afterward, people in Europe reported unusually vivid red sunsets, and average global temperatures fell slightly. These observations suggest that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["the eruption caused temperatures in Europe to rise for several years afterward.","material from the eruption spread widely through the atmosphere and affected sunlight far from Indonesia.","the vivid sunsets in Europe were caused by local weather patterns that developed independently of the eruption in Indonesia.","the eruption affected only the region immediately surrounding Krakatoa."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Red sunsets in Europe and global cooling indicate that eruption material spread worldwide and altered sunlight.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h176$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A team of researchers compared two groups of fifth-grade classrooms. In one group, teachers spent the first five minutes of each math class reviewing material from previous weeks; in the other, teachers began immediately with new material. At the end of the year, students in the review group scored higher on questions about material taught early in the year but no differently on material taught near the end. This suggests that the brief reviews ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["helped students retain older material without affecting how well they learned new material.","made it harder for students to learn the new material taught near the end of the year.","improved students' scores on all of the material covered during the year.","had no effect on how well students remembered material from early in the year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Review groups did better only on early material and the same on late material—improved retention without hurting new learning.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h177$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Medieval European maps often placed Jerusalem at the center and depicted distant regions with imaginary creatures. Historians note that these maps were rarely used for navigation; merchants and sailors relied instead on written route descriptions and charts showing coastlines and distances. This suggests that the medieval maps ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["were the main tools that merchants and sailors used to find their way between ports.","accurately depicted the geography of distant regions as it was known at the time.","were replaced by written route descriptions because they were too expensive to produce.","served purposes, such as expressing religious and cultural views, other than practical navigation."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Since navigators used other tools, and the maps centered Jerusalem and showed imaginary creatures, the maps likely expressed worldviews rather than serving navigation.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h178$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$In a series of experiments, researchers found that people who wrote briefly about their worries before a major exam performed better than those who did not. The effect was largest among students who reported high levels of test anxiety; for students with low anxiety, writing made little difference. The findings suggest that writing about worries may help by ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["teaching students new information that is directly relevant to the exam.","making students with low anxiety more anxious, which offset the benefits that the writing provided to students with high anxiety.","reducing the burden that anxious thoughts place on students' attention during the exam.","giving students extra time to complete the exam after they finished writing."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The benefit was concentrated among anxious students, suggesting writing reduced anxiety's drain on attention.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h179$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying an ancient trade route found glass beads made in Egypt at sites thousands of kilometers away, in what is now Scandinavia. The beads appeared in graves dating to around 1400 BCE, but no Egyptian objects of other kinds were found there, and no evidence suggests that Egyptians traveled that far north. The researchers proposed that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["Egyptian traders established settlements in Scandinavia around 1400 BCE.","the beads may have reached Scandinavia through a chain of trade passing through many intermediaries.","the beads were made in Scandinavia by craftspeople who had learned Egyptian glassmaking techniques from travelers.","Scandinavians traveled directly to Egypt to buy the beads and other goods."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Egyptian beads arrived without other Egyptian objects or evidence of travel, consistent with passing hand to hand through intermediaries.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h180$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$Researchers measured how much food people ate at a buffet using plates of different sizes. People given larger plates served themselves more food and ate more, even though they did not report feeling hungrier. This finding suggests that ______$q$, $q$Which choice most logically completes the text?$q$, $q$["environmental cues such as plate size can influence how much people eat independent of hunger.","people given larger plates were hungrier than people given smaller plates.","plate size has no effect on how much food people serve themselves at a buffet.","people with smaller plates ate just as much food as people with larger plates, because they returned to the buffet more often."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Larger plates led to more eating without more hunger, showing that cues influence intake.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h181$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Tensile Strength of Four Fibers (megapascals)</caption><thead><tr><th>Fiber</th><th>Dry</th><th>Wet</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fiber W</td><td>814</td><td>542</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber X</td><td>744</td><td>571</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Y</td><td>652</td><td>384</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Z</td><td>668</td><td>330</td></tr></tbody></table>A materials scientist measured the strength of four natural fibers under dry and wet conditions. Based on the results, the materials scientist concluded that Fiber X is better suited to use in wet environments than the other fibers tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under dry conditions, Fiber W had a higher strength (814) than any other fiber, including Fiber X (744).","Fiber X had a higher strength under dry conditions (744) than under wet conditions (571).","Under wet conditions, Fiber Y had a lower strength (384) than Fiber W did (542).","Under wet conditions, Fiber X had a higher strength (571) than any other fiber, and it lost the least from dry conditions to wet conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The conclusion is about performance under wet conditions relative to the other fibers. Only the first choice shows that Fiber X outperformed every other fiber under wet conditions and lost the least going from dry conditions to wet conditions. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under dry conditions, a drop that every fiber experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Fiber X.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h182$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Yield of Four Wheat Varieties (tons per hectare)</caption><thead><tr><th>Variety</th><th>Normal rainfall</th><th>Drought</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Variety K</td><td>5.3</td><td>3.7</td></tr><tr><td>Variety M</td><td>6.2</td><td>4.5</td></tr><tr><td>Variety R</td><td>5.2</td><td>4.9</td></tr><tr><td>Variety T</td><td>5.9</td><td>4.0</td></tr></tbody></table>An agronomist tested four wheat varieties under normal rainfall and under drought conditions. Based on the results, the agronomist concluded that Variety R is better suited to drought than the other varieties tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under normal rainfall, Variety M had a higher yield (6.2) than any other variety, including Variety R (5.2).","Variety R had a higher yield under normal rainfall (5.2) than under drought (4.9).","Under drought, Variety R had a higher yield (4.9) than any other variety, and it lost the least from normal rainfall to drought.","Under drought, Variety K had a lower yield (3.7) than Variety M did (4.5)."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The conclusion is about performance under drought relative to the other varieties. Only the first choice shows that Variety R outperformed every other variety under drought and lost the least going from normal rainfall to drought. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under normal rainfall, a drop that every variety experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Variety R.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h183$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Germination Rate of Seeds Stored at Two Temperatures (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Seed type</th><th>Room temperature</th><th>Freezing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type 1</td><td>89</td><td>39</td></tr><tr><td>Type 2</td><td>48</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>Type 3</td><td>70</td><td>67</td></tr><tr><td>Type 4</td><td>84</td><td>51</td></tr></tbody></table>A botanist stored seeds of four plant types at room temperature and at freezing temperatures for a year and then measured how many germinated. Based on the results, the botanist concluded that Type 3 is better suited to long-term storage at freezing temperatures than the other seed types tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under room temperature, Type 1 had a higher germination rate (89) than any other seed type, including Type 3 (70).","Under freezing, Type 3 had a higher germination rate (67) than any other seed type, and it lost the least from room temperature to freezing.","Type 3 had a higher germination rate under room temperature (70) than under freezing (67).","Under freezing, Type 2 had a lower germination rate (38) than Type 1 did (39)."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The conclusion is about performance under freezing relative to the other seed types. Only the first choice shows that Type 3 outperformed every other seed type under freezing and lost the least going from room temperature to freezing. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under room temperature, a drop that every seed type experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Type 3.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h184$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Number of Pollinator Visits per Hour to Four Flower Species</caption><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Sunny day</th><th>Cloudy day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Species P</td><td>37</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Species Q</td><td>32</td><td>29</td></tr><tr><td>Species S</td><td>24</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Species V</td><td>23</td><td>18</td></tr></tbody></table>An ecologist counted pollinator visits to four flower species on sunny and cloudy days. Based on the results, the ecologist concluded that Species Q is especially attractive to pollinators in cloudy weather than the other species tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under a cloudy day, Species Q had a higher number of visits (29) than any other species, and it lost the least from a sunny day to a cloudy day.","Under a sunny day, Species P had a higher number of visits (37) than any other species, including Species Q (32).","Species Q had a higher number of visits under a sunny day (32) than under a cloudy day (29).","Under a cloudy day, Species S had a higher number of visits (16) than Species P did (6)."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The conclusion is about performance under a cloudy day relative to the other species. Only the first choice shows that Species Q outperformed every other species under a cloudy day and lost the least going from a sunny day to a cloudy day. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under a sunny day, a drop that every species experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Species Q.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h185$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Battery Capacity Remaining After 500 Charge Cycles (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Battery design</th><th>At 20°C</th><th>At 45°C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design A</td><td>89</td><td>64</td></tr><tr><td>Design B</td><td>95</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Design C</td><td>88</td><td>82</td></tr><tr><td>Design D</td><td>73</td><td>46</td></tr></tbody></table>An engineer tested four battery designs by charging and discharging them 500 times at 20°C and at 45°C. Based on the results, the engineer concluded that Design C is better suited to hot climates than the other battery designs tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under 20°C, Design B had a higher remaining capacity (95) than any other battery design, including Design C (88).","Design C had a higher remaining capacity under 20°C (88) than under 45°C (82).","Under 45°C, Design A had a higher remaining capacity (64) than Design B did (60).","Under 45°C, Design C had a higher remaining capacity (82) than any other battery design, and it lost the least from 20°C to 45°C."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The conclusion is about performance under 45°C relative to the other battery designs. Only the first choice shows that Design C outperformed every other battery design under 45°C and lost the least going from 20°C to 45°C. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under 20°C, a drop that every battery design experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Design C.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h186$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Number of Pollinator Visits per Hour to Four Flower Species</caption><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Sunny day</th><th>Cloudy day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Species P</td><td>45</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>Species Q</td><td>30</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>Species S</td><td>33</td><td>28</td></tr><tr><td>Species V</td><td>42</td><td>17</td></tr></tbody></table>An ecologist counted pollinator visits to four flower species on sunny and cloudy days. Based on the results, the ecologist concluded that Species S is especially attractive to pollinators in cloudy weather than the other species tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under a sunny day, Species P had a higher number of visits (45) than any other species, including Species S (33).","Species S had a higher number of visits under a sunny day (33) than under a cloudy day (28).","Under a cloudy day, Species S had a higher number of visits (28) than any other species, and it lost the least from a sunny day to a cloudy day.","Under a cloudy day, Species Q had a higher number of visits (23) than Species P did (11)."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The conclusion is about performance under a cloudy day relative to the other species. Only the first choice shows that Species S outperformed every other species under a cloudy day and lost the least going from a sunny day to a cloudy day. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under a sunny day, a drop that every species experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Species S.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h187$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Number of Pollinator Visits per Hour to Four Flower Species</caption><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Sunny day</th><th>Cloudy day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Species P</td><td>29</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Species Q</td><td>35</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>Species S</td><td>32</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Species V</td><td>34</td><td>16</td></tr></tbody></table>An ecologist counted pollinator visits to four flower species on sunny and cloudy days. Based on the results, the ecologist concluded that Species P is especially attractive to pollinators in cloudy weather than the other species tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under a sunny day, Species Q had a higher number of visits (35) than any other species, including Species P (29).","Under a cloudy day, Species P had a higher number of visits (19) than any other species, and it lost the least from a sunny day to a cloudy day.","Species P had a higher number of visits under a sunny day (29) than under a cloudy day (19).","Under a cloudy day, Species S had a lower number of visits (13) than Species Q did (18)."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The conclusion is about performance under a cloudy day relative to the other species. Only the first choice shows that Species P outperformed every other species under a cloudy day and lost the least going from a sunny day to a cloudy day. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under a sunny day, a drop that every species experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Species P.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h188$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Reading Comprehension Scores Under Two Conditions (out of 100)</caption><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Silence</th><th>Background music</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Group 1</td><td>88</td><td>75</td></tr><tr><td>Group 2</td><td>93</td><td>62</td></tr><tr><td>Group 3</td><td>84</td><td>53</td></tr><tr><td>Group 4</td><td>90</td><td>64</td></tr></tbody></table>A psychologist tested four groups of students who used different study methods, measuring reading comprehension in silence and with background music. Based on the results, the psychologist concluded that Group 1 is especially resistant to distraction from music than the other groups tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under background music, Group 1 had a higher score (75) than any other group, and it lost the least from silence to background music.","Under silence, Group 2 had a higher score (93) than any other group, including Group 1 (88).","Group 1 had a higher score under silence (88) than under background music (75).","Under background music, Group 3 had a lower score (53) than Group 2 did (62)."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The conclusion is about performance under background music relative to the other groups. Only the first choice shows that Group 1 outperformed every other group under background music and lost the least going from silence to background music. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under silence, a drop that every group experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Group 1.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h189$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Germination Rate of Seeds Stored at Two Temperatures (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Seed type</th><th>Room temperature</th><th>Freezing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type 1</td><td>76</td><td>37</td></tr><tr><td>Type 2</td><td>48</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>Type 3</td><td>84</td><td>52</td></tr><tr><td>Type 4</td><td>64</td><td>55</td></tr></tbody></table>A botanist stored seeds of four plant types at room temperature and at freezing temperatures for a year and then measured how many germinated. Based on the results, the botanist concluded that Type 4 is better suited to long-term storage at freezing temperatures than the other seed types tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under room temperature, Type 3 had a higher germination rate (84) than any other seed type, including Type 4 (64).","Type 4 had a higher germination rate under room temperature (64) than under freezing (55).","Under freezing, Type 1 had a lower germination rate (37) than Type 3 did (52).","Under freezing, Type 4 had a higher germination rate (55) than any other seed type, and it lost the least from room temperature to freezing."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The conclusion is about performance under freezing relative to the other seed types. Only the first choice shows that Type 4 outperformed every other seed type under freezing and lost the least going from room temperature to freezing. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under room temperature, a drop that every seed type experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Type 4.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h190$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Battery Capacity Remaining After 500 Charge Cycles (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Battery design</th><th>At 20°C</th><th>At 45°C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design A</td><td>63</td><td>57</td></tr><tr><td>Design B</td><td>77</td><td>74</td></tr><tr><td>Design C</td><td>87</td><td>52</td></tr><tr><td>Design D</td><td>96</td><td>72</td></tr></tbody></table>An engineer tested four battery designs by charging and discharging them 500 times at 20°C and at 45°C. Based on the results, the engineer concluded that Design B is better suited to hot climates than the other battery designs tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under 20°C, Design D had a higher remaining capacity (96) than any other battery design, including Design B (77).","Design B had a higher remaining capacity under 20°C (77) than under 45°C (74).","Under 45°C, Design B had a higher remaining capacity (74) than any other battery design, and it lost the least from 20°C to 45°C.","Under 45°C, Design A had a lower remaining capacity (57) than Design D did (72)."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The conclusion is about performance under 45°C relative to the other battery designs. Only the first choice shows that Design B outperformed every other battery design under 45°C and lost the least going from 20°C to 45°C. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under 20°C, a drop that every battery design experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Design B.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h191$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Battery Capacity Remaining After 500 Charge Cycles (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Battery design</th><th>At 20°C</th><th>At 45°C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design A</td><td>55</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>Design B</td><td>81</td><td>73</td></tr><tr><td>Design C</td><td>95</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Design D</td><td>54</td><td>45</td></tr></tbody></table>An engineer tested four battery designs by charging and discharging them 500 times at 20°C and at 45°C. Based on the results, the engineer concluded that Design B is better suited to hot climates than the other battery designs tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under 20°C, Design C had a higher remaining capacity (95) than any other battery design, including Design B (81).","Under 45°C, Design B had a higher remaining capacity (73) than any other battery design, and it lost the least from 20°C to 45°C.","Design B had a higher remaining capacity under 20°C (81) than under 45°C (73).","Under 45°C, Design A had a lower remaining capacity (40) than Design C did (60)."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The conclusion is about performance under 45°C relative to the other battery designs. Only the first choice shows that Design B outperformed every other battery design under 45°C and lost the least going from 20°C to 45°C. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under 20°C, a drop that every battery design experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Design B.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h192$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Battery Capacity Remaining After 500 Charge Cycles (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Battery design</th><th>At 20°C</th><th>At 45°C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design A</td><td>94</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>Design B</td><td>96</td><td>76</td></tr><tr><td>Design C</td><td>90</td><td>85</td></tr><tr><td>Design D</td><td>91</td><td>65</td></tr></tbody></table>An engineer tested four battery designs by charging and discharging them 500 times at 20°C and at 45°C. Based on the results, the engineer concluded that Design C is better suited to hot climates than the other battery designs tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under 45°C, Design C had a higher remaining capacity (85) than any other battery design, and it lost the least from 20°C to 45°C.","Under 20°C, Design B had a higher remaining capacity (96) than any other battery design, including Design C (90).","Design C had a higher remaining capacity under 20°C (90) than under 45°C (85).","Under 45°C, Design A had a lower remaining capacity (50) than Design B did (76)."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The conclusion is about performance under 45°C relative to the other battery designs. Only the first choice shows that Design C outperformed every other battery design under 45°C and lost the least going from 20°C to 45°C. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under 20°C, a drop that every battery design experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Design C.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h193$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Battery Capacity Remaining After 500 Charge Cycles (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Battery design</th><th>At 20°C</th><th>At 45°C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design A</td><td>86</td><td>45</td></tr><tr><td>Design B</td><td>69</td><td>67</td></tr><tr><td>Design C</td><td>83</td><td>57</td></tr><tr><td>Design D</td><td>87</td><td>43</td></tr></tbody></table>An engineer tested four battery designs by charging and discharging them 500 times at 20°C and at 45°C. Based on the results, the engineer concluded that Design B is better suited to hot climates than the other battery designs tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under 20°C, Design D had a higher remaining capacity (87) than any other battery design, including Design B (69).","Design B had a higher remaining capacity under 20°C (69) than under 45°C (67).","Under 45°C, Design A had a higher remaining capacity (45) than Design D did (43).","Under 45°C, Design B had a higher remaining capacity (67) than any other battery design, and it lost the least from 20°C to 45°C."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The conclusion is about performance under 45°C relative to the other battery designs. Only the first choice shows that Design B outperformed every other battery design under 45°C and lost the least going from 20°C to 45°C. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under 20°C, a drop that every battery design experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Design B.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h194$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Reading Comprehension Scores Under Two Conditions (out of 100)</caption><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Silence</th><th>Background music</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Group 1</td><td>79</td><td>63</td></tr><tr><td>Group 2</td><td>72</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Group 3</td><td>94</td><td>76</td></tr><tr><td>Group 4</td><td>93</td><td>91</td></tr></tbody></table>A psychologist tested four groups of students who used different study methods, measuring reading comprehension in silence and with background music. Based on the results, the psychologist concluded that Group 4 is especially resistant to distraction from music than the other groups tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under silence, Group 3 had a higher score (94) than any other group, including Group 4 (93).","Group 4 had a higher score under silence (93) than under background music (91).","Under background music, Group 4 had a higher score (91) than any other group, and it lost the least from silence to background music.","Under background music, Group 1 had a lower score (63) than Group 3 did (76)."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The conclusion is about performance under background music relative to the other groups. Only the first choice shows that Group 4 outperformed every other group under background music and lost the least going from silence to background music. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under silence, a drop that every group experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Group 4.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h195$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Yield of Four Wheat Varieties (tons per hectare)</caption><thead><tr><th>Variety</th><th>Normal rainfall</th><th>Drought</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Variety K</td><td>4.0</td><td>2.8</td></tr><tr><td>Variety M</td><td>2.8</td><td>2.5</td></tr><tr><td>Variety R</td><td>3.1</td><td>3.0</td></tr><tr><td>Variety T</td><td>5.3</td><td>2.1</td></tr></tbody></table>An agronomist tested four wheat varieties under normal rainfall and under drought conditions. Based on the results, the agronomist concluded that Variety R is better suited to drought than the other varieties tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under normal rainfall, Variety T had a higher yield (5.3) than any other variety, including Variety R (3.1).","Under drought, Variety R had a higher yield (3.0) than any other variety, and it lost the least from normal rainfall to drought.","Variety R had a higher yield under normal rainfall (3.1) than under drought (3.0).","Under drought, Variety K had a higher yield (2.8) than Variety T did (2.1)."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The conclusion is about performance under drought relative to the other varieties. Only the first choice shows that Variety R outperformed every other variety under drought and lost the least going from normal rainfall to drought. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under normal rainfall, a drop that every variety experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Variety R.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h196$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Germination Rate of Seeds Stored at Two Temperatures (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Seed type</th><th>Room temperature</th><th>Freezing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type 1</td><td>91</td><td>61</td></tr><tr><td>Type 2</td><td>81</td><td>78</td></tr><tr><td>Type 3</td><td>71</td><td>48</td></tr><tr><td>Type 4</td><td>94</td><td>43</td></tr></tbody></table>A botanist stored seeds of four plant types at room temperature and at freezing temperatures for a year and then measured how many germinated. Based on the results, the botanist concluded that Type 2 is better suited to long-term storage at freezing temperatures than the other seed types tested.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the conclusion?$q$, $q$["Under freezing, Type 2 had a higher germination rate (78) than any other seed type, and it lost the least from room temperature to freezing.","Under room temperature, Type 4 had a higher germination rate (94) than any other seed type, including Type 2 (81).","Type 2 had a higher germination rate under room temperature (81) than under freezing (78).","Under freezing, Type 1 had a higher germination rate (61) than Type 4 did (43)."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The conclusion is about performance under freezing relative to the other seed types. Only the first choice shows that Type 2 outperformed every other seed type under freezing and lost the least going from room temperature to freezing. The other choices are accurate but describe performance under room temperature, a drop that every seed type experienced, or a comparison that doesn't involve Type 2.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h197$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Tensile Strength of Four Fibers (megapascals)</caption><thead><tr><th>Fiber</th><th>Dry</th><th>Wet</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fiber W</td><td>472</td><td>305</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber X</td><td>293</td><td>247</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Y</td><td>786</td><td>789</td></tr><tr><td>Fiber Z</td><td>549</td><td>370</td></tr></tbody></table>A materials scientist measured the strength of four natural fibers under dry and wet conditions. A colleague claimed that every fiber tested had a lower strength under wet conditions than under dry conditions.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that weaken the colleague's claim?$q$, $q$["Fiber Z had a strength of 549 under dry conditions, compared with 370 under wet conditions.","Fiber X had the lowest strength of any fiber under wet conditions, at 247.","Fiber W had a strength of 472 under dry conditions, lower than that of Fiber Y under the same conditions.","Fiber Y had a strength of 789 under wet conditions, compared with 786 under dry conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The claim is that every fiber declined under wet conditions. Fiber Y is a counterexample: its strength rose from 786 to 789. The other choices describe declines or comparisons that are consistent with the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h198$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Average Number of Pollinator Visits per Hour to Four Flower Species</caption><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Sunny day</th><th>Cloudy day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Species P</td><td>10</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Species Q</td><td>19</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Species S</td><td>20</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Species V</td><td>38</td><td>42</td></tr></tbody></table>An ecologist counted pollinator visits to four flower species on sunny and cloudy days. A colleague claimed that every species tested had a lower number of visits under a cloudy day than under a sunny day.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that weaken the colleague's claim?$q$, $q$["Species P had a number of visits of 10 under a sunny day, compared with 4 under a cloudy day.","Species P had the lowest number of visits of any species under a cloudy day, at 4.","Species V had a number of visits of 42 under a cloudy day, compared with 38 under a sunny day.","Species Q had a number of visits of 19 under a sunny day, lower than that of Species V under the same conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The claim is that every species declined under a cloudy day. Species V is a counterexample: its number of visits rose from 38 to 42. The other choices describe declines or comparisons that are consistent with the claim.$q$),
