  ($q$ii-h210$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Germination Rate of Seeds Stored at Two Temperatures (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Seed type</th><th>Room temperature</th><th>Freezing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Type 1</td><td>63</td><td>65</td></tr><tr><td>Type 2</td><td>95</td><td>81</td></tr><tr><td>Type 3</td><td>37</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Type 4</td><td>36</td><td>33</td></tr></tbody></table>A botanist stored seeds of four plant types at room temperature and at freezing temperatures for a year and then measured how many germinated. A colleague claimed that every seed type tested had a lower germination rate under freezing than under room temperature.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that weaken the colleague's claim?$q$, $q$["Type 2 had a germination rate of 95 under room temperature, compared with 81 under freezing.","Type 3 had the lowest germination rate of any seed type under freezing, at 30.","Type 1 had a germination rate of 65 under freezing, compared with 63 under room temperature.","Type 4 had a germination rate of 36 under room temperature, lower than that of Type 1 under the same conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The claim is that every seed type declined under freezing. Type 1 is a counterexample: its germination rate rose from 63 to 65. The other choices describe declines or comparisons that are consistent with the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h211$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Battery Capacity Remaining After 500 Charge Cycles (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Battery design</th><th>At 20°C</th><th>At 45°C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design A</td><td>40</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>Design B</td><td>87</td><td>73</td></tr><tr><td>Design C</td><td>54</td><td>63</td></tr><tr><td>Design D</td><td>62</td><td>58</td></tr></tbody></table>An engineer tested four battery designs by charging and discharging them 500 times at 20°C and at 45°C. A colleague claimed that every battery design tested had a lower remaining capacity under 45°C than under 20°C.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that weaken the colleague's claim?$q$, $q$["Design B had a remaining capacity of 87 under 20°C, compared with 73 under 45°C.","Design C had a remaining capacity of 63 under 45°C, compared with 54 under 20°C.","Design A had the lowest remaining capacity of any battery design under 45°C, at 40.","Design D had a remaining capacity of 62 under 20°C, higher than that of Design C under the same conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The claim is that every battery design declined under 45°C. Design C is a counterexample: its remaining capacity rose from 54 to 63. The other choices describe declines or comparisons that are consistent with the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h212$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Battery Capacity Remaining After 500 Charge Cycles (%)</caption><thead><tr><th>Battery design</th><th>At 20°C</th><th>At 45°C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Design A</td><td>59</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>Design B</td><td>40</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>Design C</td><td>89</td><td>94</td></tr><tr><td>Design D</td><td>78</td><td>75</td></tr></tbody></table>An engineer tested four battery designs by charging and discharging them 500 times at 20°C and at 45°C. A colleague claimed that every battery design tested had a lower remaining capacity under 45°C than under 20°C.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes data from the table that weaken the colleague's claim?$q$, $q$["Design C had a remaining capacity of 94 under 45°C, compared with 89 under 20°C.","Design A had a remaining capacity of 59 under 20°C, compared with 50 under 45°C.","Design B had the lowest remaining capacity of any battery design under 45°C, at 40.","Design D had a remaining capacity of 78 under 20°C, lower than that of Design C under the same conditions."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim is that every battery design declined under 45°C. Design C is a counterexample: its remaining capacity rose from 89 to 94. The other choices describe declines or comparisons that are consistent with the claim.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h213$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Books Checked Out at Four Library Branches (thousands)</caption><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>2016</th><th>2024</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>North Branch</td><td>48</td><td>85</td></tr><tr><td>South Branch</td><td>101</td><td>201</td></tr><tr><td>East Branch</td><td>55</td><td>84</td></tr><tr><td>West Branch</td><td>57</td><td>125</td></tr></tbody></table>A city report examined changes in the number of books checked out between 2016 and 2024. The report noted that, relative to its 2016 level, West Branch grew more than any of the other branches listed.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the report's claim?$q$, $q$["South Branch's number of books checked out rose by 100, a larger increase in absolute terms than that of any other branch in the table.","In 2024, South Branch had the highest number of books checked out of any branch in the table, at 201.","West Branch's number of books checked out in 2016 (57) was lower than South Branch's number of books checked out in 2016 (101).","West Branch's number of books checked out rose from 57 to 125, an increase of about 119%, the largest percentage increase of any branch in the table."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The claim concerns growth relative to the 2016 level—percentage growth. West Branch grew by about 119%, more than any other. South Branch had the largest absolute increase, which is a different measure, and the other choices don't address growth relative to the starting level.$q$),
  ($q$ii-h214$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$<table class="data-table"><caption>Nesting Pairs of Four Seabird Species on an Island</caption><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>2000</th><th>2020</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Puffins</td><td>418</td><td>847</td></tr><tr><td>Terns</td><td>130</td><td>282</td></tr><tr><td>Gulls</td><td>157</td><td>310</td></tr><tr><td>Kittiwakes</td><td>484</td><td>665</td></tr></tbody></table>A city report examined changes in the number of nesting pairs between 2000 and 2020. The report noted that, relative to its 2000 level, Terns grew more than any of the other species listed.$q$, $q$Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the report's claim?$q$, $q$["Puffins's number of nesting pairs rose by 429, a larger increase in absolute terms than that of any other species in the table.","In 2020, Puffins had the highest number of nesting pairs of any species in the table, at 847.","Terns's number of nesting pairs rose from 130 to 282, an increase of about 117%, the largest percentage increase of any species in the table.","Terns's number of nesting pairs in 2000 (130) was lower than Puffins's number of nesting pairs in 2000 (418)."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The claim concerns growth relative to the 2000 level—percentage growth. Terns grew by about 117%, more than any other. Puffins had the largest absolute increase, which is a different measure, and the other choices don't address growth relative to the starting level.$q$),
