Text 2: Economist B argues that office demand is cyclical and will rebound as companies mandate return-to-office policies, citing recent upticks in leasing activity in several major cities.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Economist B most likely respond to Economist A's claim?$q$, $q$["By arguing that the elevated vacancy rates reflect a temporary cycle rather than a permanent shift","By agreeing that office demand has disappeared permanently","By dismissing all vacancy rate data as irrelevant to the discussion","By claiming remote work never affected office demand at all"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Economist B's position is that demand is cyclical, not permanently reduced, so B would frame A's elevated vacancy rates as part of a temporary cycle rather than a lasting shift.$q$),
  ($q$cs-24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Historian A contends that the fall of the trade city was primarily caused by a shift in maritime trade routes away from its harbor.

Text 2: Historian B contends that internal political corruption, documented in surviving tax records, was the primary cause of the city's decline, occurring even before trade routes shifted.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two historians' views?$q$, $q$["Historian A emphasizes an external economic cause, while Historian B emphasizes an internal political cause.","Historian B treats the political corruption as a consequence of the trade route shift rather than an independent cause.","Historian B relies exclusively on maritime trade records.","Historian A blames the decline on political corruption."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Historian A points to trade routes (an external, economic factor), while Historian B points to corruption that occurred 'even before trade routes shifted' — an independent, internal cause, not a downstream consequence — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Nutrition researcher A found that participants on a high-protein breakfast reported feeling full longer than those on a high-carbohydrate breakfast.

Text 2: Nutrition researcher B found no significant difference in reported fullness between high-protein and high-carbohydrate breakfasts, though her study used a considerably smaller sample size.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two researchers?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's results do not confirm Researcher A's findings, though the smaller sample size may limit that comparison.","Researcher B's findings exactly replicate Researcher A's results.","Researcher B's larger sample size makes her null result more reliable than Researcher A's.","Researcher A found no difference between the two meal types."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Researcher B's null result differs from Researcher A's positive result, but the passage notes B's sample size was considerably smaller, not larger, a detail that qualifies rather than strengthens the comparison.$q$),
  ($q$cs-26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The critic praised the film's ______ pacing, noting that not a single scene felt rushed or drawn out.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["measured","erratic","frantic","careless"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pacing is described as never feeling 'rushed or drawn out,' meaning it was carefully controlled — 'measured' captures that balance.$q$),
  ($q$cs-27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$The article opens by describing a single beekeeper's declining honey yields, then broadens to examine regional pollinator population data, and finally proposes several conservation policies.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a specific example to broader evidence to proposed solutions.","It presents two competing theories and evaluates each in turn.","It begins with a general policy proposal and ends with a specific anecdote.","It compares beekeeping practices in two different countries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text follows a clear progression: one beekeeper's story, then regional data, then policy proposals — moving from specific to general to solution-oriented.$q$),
  ($q$cs-28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Despite the coach's ______ demeanor during practice, players said she became remarkably calm and focused once a game actually began.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["intense","relaxed","indifferent","calm"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Despite' signals a contrast with the calm, focused demeanor described in games, so her practice demeanor must be the opposite — 'intense.'$q$),
  ($q$cs-29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Urban economist A argues that rising downtown rents are driven primarily by increased demand from remote workers seeking city amenities without daily commutes.

Text 2: Urban economist B argues that rising downtown rents are driven primarily by restrictive zoning laws that have limited new housing construction for decades, regardless of recent shifts in work patterns.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how Economist B would likely respond to Economist A's argument?$q$, $q$["B would contend that zoning restrictions, not recent remote-work trends, are the more fundamental cause of rising rents.","B would agree entirely that remote work is the primary driver of rent increases.","B would argue that rents have not actually increased in most cities.","B would claim that zoning laws were only recently enacted."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Economist B attributes the trend to long-standing zoning restrictions 'regardless of recent shifts in work patterns,' directly countering A's remote-work explanation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A profile of a retired engineer begins with an extended account of a bridge collapse she witnessed as a child, an event she says first drew her to structural engineering. The piece then details her decades-long career, and closes by returning to a bridge — this time one she designed — noting the echo between the two structures without stating it directly.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the closing paragraph functions in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It creates a deliberate parallel with the opening anecdote, implicitly connecting her early experience to her later achievement.","It introduces a new critique of the engineer's design choices.","It introduces a bridge designed by a different engineer for contrast.","It contradicts the claim made in the opening paragraph."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The closing paragraph revisits a bridge she herself designed, echoing the opening 'without stating it directly,' which links her origin story to her own career, not a different engineer's work, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Rather than refuting her colleague's data outright, the scientist offered a more ______ critique, suggesting the sample size was too small to support such a broad conclusion.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["measured","scathing","cautious","hostile"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Rather than refuting...outright' signals restraint in tone, not uncertainty about the critique itself — 'measured' conveys a deliberately controlled, proportionate tone, while 'cautious' would suggest she herself was unsure of the critique, which her confident claim about sample size contradicts.$q$),
  ($q$cs-32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: A film critic argues that a director's use of long, uninterrupted takes creates a sense of realism that quick editing cannot match.

Text 2: A film editor counters that skillful editing can create an even stronger illusion of continuous time than an actual unbroken take, since editing lets a director remove imperfections.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?$q$, $q$["Text 2 challenges the assumption in Text 1 that long takes are inherently more effective at conveying realism.","Text 2 fully agrees with the claim made in Text 1.","Text 1 and Text 2 both argue that editing should be avoided entirely.","Text 2 focuses on a completely unrelated aspect of filmmaking."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Text 2 directly disputes Text 1's premise, arguing editing can achieve what Text 1 claims only unedited long takes can — a direct challenge to Text 1's assumption.$q$),
  ($q$cs-33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The essay's final paragraph returns to a question posed in the first paragraph, this time answering it directly after several paragraphs of supporting evidence.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the final paragraph?$q$, $q$["It resolves the question raised at the start of the essay, now supported by the evidence presented.","It introduces a brand-new argument unrelated to the rest of the essay.","It undermines the evidence presented earlier in the essay.","It restates the essay's introduction without adding new information."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The final paragraph directly answers the question from the introduction, now backed by the evidence built up through the essay — a resolving function.$q$),
  ($q$cs-34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Amateur astronomers were initially ______ about the newly reported comet, since similar sightings in the past had turned out to be reflections from satellites.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["doubtful","delighted","indifferent","certain"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Past false sightings would reasonably make astronomers doubtful of a new report, not delighted or certain about it.$q$),
  ($q$cs-35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on glassblowing opens with a description of molten glass being gathered on a blowpipe, then explains the physics of viscosity that makes the technique possible, and closes by noting how modern glassblowers still rely on centuries-old hand gestures.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a vivid process description to scientific explanation to a reflection on tradition.","It presents two rival theories of glass chemistry and refutes one of them.","It opens with a conclusion and works backward to the evidence supporting it.","It compares glassblowing to an unrelated craft throughout the article."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The article's stated progression — process, then physics, then a closing reflection on tradition — matches choice A exactly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Ornithologist A argues that migratory songbirds primarily rely on the Earth's magnetic field to navigate during long flights, citing displacement experiments in which birds still found their route after being moved off course.

Text 2: Ornithologist B argues that star patterns are the dominant cue, citing that birds raised under artificial night skies later misjudge migratory direction accordingly.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two ornithologists' views?$q$, $q$["Ornithologist A emphasizes a magnetic cue, while Ornithologist B emphasizes a celestial cue.","Both ornithologists agree that magnetic fields are irrelevant to migration.","Ornithologist B relies exclusively on displacement experiments.","Ornithologist A argues that star patterns guide migration."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Ornithologist A points to the magnetic field, while Ornithologist B points to star patterns — the physical-versus-celestial contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Marine biologists described the reef's recovery as ______, noting that coral cover rebounded to pre-bleaching levels within just two years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["remarkable","negligible","predictable","artificial"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Coral cover rebounding to prior levels within only two years is a notably fast recovery, which 'remarkable' captures.$q$),
  ($q$cs-38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A biography of a jazz pianist spends its opening chapter on her childhood piano lessons, then devotes a full chapter to a single recording session that critics call her masterpiece, treating it as the turning point of her career.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the chapter devoted to the recording session?$q$, $q$["It highlights a pivotal moment that the biography treats as central to her artistic development.","It disproves the claims made earlier about her childhood lessons.","It shifts entirely away from music to focus on her personal life.","It summarizes her whole career before any details are given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text explicitly frames the recording session as 'the turning point of her career,' so the chapter functions to highlight a pivotal moment, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Translator A argues that a translation should prioritize preserving the original's exact word order and syntax, even if the resulting English sounds unusual, so readers experience the source language's rhythm.

Text 2: Translator B argues that a translation should prioritize natural, fluent English, contending that awkward syntax distracts readers from the meaning the original author intended to convey.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Translator B most likely respond to Translator A's approach?$q$, $q$["By arguing that preserving unusual syntax distracts readers from the author's intended meaning.","By agreeing that unusual syntax is the most important goal of translation.","By claiming that word order never affects a reader's experience.","By stating that fluency is irrelevant to good translation."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Translator B's stated position — that awkward syntax distracts from meaning — directly counters Translator A's preference for preserving unusual syntax.$q$),
  ($q$cs-40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The volcanologist's report was notably ______, avoiding any firm prediction and instead listing several equally plausible outcomes for the volcano's activity over the coming months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["hedged","definitive","alarmist","ambiguous"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Listing several explicit, equally plausible outcomes is a deliberate hedge against being wrong, not mere vagueness — an ambiguous report would be unclear about what it means, whereas this one is clear but noncommittal.$q$),
  ($q$cs-41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on the history of the typewriter spends its first two paragraphs celebrating the machine's role in expanding clerical employment for women in the late nineteenth century, then devotes its final paragraph to noting that the same machines standardized and constrained the pace at which those workers were expected to type.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the final paragraph in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It complicates the earlier celebration by introducing a less positive consequence of the same technology.","It repeats the praise offered in the first two paragraphs without adding anything new.","It shifts focus to how factory workers, rather than clerical workers, experienced the same standardization.","It proves that the typewriter had no effect on women's employment."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After celebrating the typewriter's benefits, the final paragraph introduces a constraining effect on the same clerical workers just celebrated, complicating rather than repeating the earlier praise or shifting to a different group entirely.$q$),
  ($q$cs-42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Paleontologist A argues that a single asteroid impact was the primary cause of the mass extinction, citing an iridium layer found worldwide at the same geological boundary.

Text 2: Paleontologist B argues that prolonged volcanic activity over hundreds of thousands of years was the primary cause, citing that many species were already declining before the iridium layer was deposited.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two paleontologists' views?$q$, $q$["Paleontologist A points to a sudden single event, while Paleontologist B points to a gradual, prolonged process.","Paleontologist A attributes the extinction to a gradual period of volcanic activity rather than a sudden impact.","Paleontologist B relies exclusively on the iridium layer.","Paleontologist A argues that species were declining long before the extinction."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Paleontologist A attributes the extinction to a sudden asteroid impact, while Paleontologist B attributes it to gradual volcanic decline, matching choice A; the second choice swaps that pairing, wrongly crediting the gradual explanation to A instead of B.$q$),
  ($q$cs-43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The new conductor's rehearsals were ______, running exactly sixty minutes with no time wasted on repeated passages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["efficient","chaotic","leisurely","unplanned"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rehearsals that run exactly sixty minutes with no wasted time describe an efficient process, not a chaotic or leisurely one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on early cartography begins by describing a medieval map that places Jerusalem at the center of the world, then explains that such maps prioritized religious symbolism over geographic accuracy.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains the reasoning behind the unusual feature described in the first sentence.","It contradicts the description given in the first sentence.","It introduces a completely unrelated map from a different era.","It proves that medieval maps were more accurate than modern ones."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence accounts for why Jerusalem was placed at the center — religious symbolism over accuracy — explaining the feature the first sentence describes.$q$),
  ($q$cs-45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Food scientist A found that fermenting vegetables at a lower temperature produced a milder, less acidic flavor preferred by most tasters in her study.

Text 2: Food scientist B found that fermentation temperature made no noticeable difference to taste in his study, though his tasting panel was much smaller than Scientist A's.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two scientists?$q$, $q$["Scientist B's results do not confirm Scientist A's findings, though the smaller panel size may limit that comparison.","Scientist B's findings exactly replicate Scientist A's results.","Both scientists studied baking rather than fermentation.","Scientist A found that temperature made no difference in taste."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Scientist B's null result differs from Scientist A's positive result, but the passage notes B's smaller panel, a detail that qualifies rather than fully undermines the comparison.$q$),
  ($q$cs-46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Despite brutal conditions, the expedition's supply chain remained ______, delivering fuel and food to each camp exactly on schedule for the entire six-month journey.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["reliable","erratic","delayed","improvised"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Delivering supplies exactly on schedule for six months despite brutal conditions describes a reliable supply chain, not an erratic or delayed one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A museum essay on medieval stained glass opens by describing the vivid colors visible from inside a cathedral, then explains that many of these windows were originally designed to be read as a sequence, telling a biblical story from left to right across the nave.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It reveals a structural, narrative purpose behind the windows that goes beyond their visual color.","It disputes the claim that the windows are colorful.","It argues that the windows were purely decorative with no narrative purpose.","It shifts the essay's focus to an unrelated medieval artform."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence moves beyond the windows' color to explain their sequential storytelling function, adding a structural purpose rather than disputing or ignoring the first sentence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Marine biologist A argues that satellite tagging shows individual whales returning to the same feeding grounds year after year, suggesting strong site fidelity.

Text 2: Marine biologist B argues that some tagged whales shifted feeding grounds substantially within a single decade, suggesting migration routes are more flexible than previously believed.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Marine biologist B most likely respond to Marine biologist A's claim?$q$, $q$["By pointing to observed shifts in feeding grounds as evidence that migration routes are not as fixed as A suggests.","By agreeing that all whales return to identical feeding grounds every year.","By arguing that satellite tagging cannot track whales at all.","By claiming that whales do not migrate in any consistent pattern."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Marine biologist B's finding of substantial shifts in feeding grounds directly counters A's claim of strong site fidelity, matching choice A's description of the response.$q$),
  ($q$cs-49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Rather than following the rigid conventions of formal calligraphy, the artist developed a style so ______ that no two of her letters were ever formed exactly the same way twice.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["idiosyncratic","standardized","illegible","unconventional"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The style is defined by variation within itself — no two letters alike — which 'idiosyncratic' captures as a personal peculiarity; 'unconventional' only means it departs from formal norms and could still describe a style that stays internally consistent.$q$),
  ($q$cs-50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A history of cryptography devotes its opening section to ciphers used in ancient warfare, its middle section to the mechanical codebreaking machines of the twentieth century, and its final section to a discussion of the mathematical problems that make modern encryption difficult to break.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It traces the development of a practice chronologically from ancient methods to modern mathematical foundations.","It presents a single case study and analyzes it from multiple perspectives.","It begins with modern encryption and works backward to ancient ciphers.","It argues that ancient ciphers were more secure than modern encryption."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The three sections move in chronological order from ancient ciphers to twentieth-century machines to modern mathematics, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Historian A argues that lighthouse automation in the twentieth century was driven primarily by advances in electrical engineering that made unmanned lights reliable.

Text 2: Historian B argues that automation was driven primarily by the rising cost of paying human keepers, noting that the necessary technology existed for years before it was widely adopted for that financial reason.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two historians' views?$q$, $q$["Historian A emphasizes a technological cause, while Historian B emphasizes an economic cause.","Both historians agree the technology was unavailable until the twentieth century.","Historian B argues that engineering advances were irrelevant to automation.","Historian A claims that cost was the primary driver of automation."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Historian A points to engineering advances (a technological cause), while Historian B points to the cost of paying keepers (an economic cause) — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The exhibit praised the quilt's ______ pattern, in which the same small triangle repeated hundreds of times to form a single unbroken design.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["repetitive","random","faded","asymmetrical"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A single small triangle repeated 'hundreds of times' to form the design describes a repetitive pattern, not a random or asymmetrical one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A report on a retreating glacier opens with striking photographs taken forty years apart, then presents temperature and ice-core data collected over the same period to explain the visible change.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It supplies quantitative evidence that accounts for the visual change shown in the photographs.","It contradicts the visual evidence shown in the photographs.","It introduces an unrelated glacier in a different region.","It concludes the report before any data has been presented."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The temperature and ice-core data are presented specifically to explain the change the photographs show, functioning as supporting evidence rather than contradiction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Mycologist A recommends identifying wild mushrooms primarily by spore print color, arguing it is the most reliable single trait for distinguishing similar-looking species.

Text 2: Mycologist B recommends relying on a combination of habitat, smell, and cap texture, arguing that spore prints alone can be misleading for beginners without a microscope.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two mycologists' recommendations?$q$, $q$["Mycologist B favors a multi-trait approach over the single method Mycologist A considers most reliable.","Both mycologists agree that spore prints should never be used.","Mycologist A recommends relying only on smell and texture.","Mycologist B argues that habitat is irrelevant to identification."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Mycologist B's combination approach is offered as an alternative to the single-trait method Mycologist A recommends, matching the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The artisan's papermaking technique remained ______ despite decades of industrial automation elsewhere, as she still pulled each sheet by hand from a wooden frame.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["traditional","mechanized","obsolete","digital"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Pulling each sheet by hand from a wooden frame, despite automation elsewhere, describes a traditional technique, not a mechanized or digital one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on the Silk Road opens by listing the goods that traveled along it — silk, spices, glassware — then argues that ideas and technologies, not just objects, moved along the same routes, and spends its remaining paragraphs tracing how papermaking techniques spread westward this way.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To broaden a narrow view of the Silk Road as only a route for physical goods","To argue that the Silk Road carried no physical goods at all","To provide a complete inventory of every good traded historically","To criticize papermaking as an inferior technology"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay starts with goods, then explicitly argues for a broader view that includes ideas and technologies, matching choice A's description of the purpose.$q$),
  ($q$cs-57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Perfumer A argues that synthetic fragrance compounds can replicate natural scents so precisely that trained noses cannot reliably tell the difference in blind tests.

Text 2: Perfumer B argues that natural extracts retain a subtle complexity that synthetic compounds still lack, citing blind tests in which expert panels consistently identified the synthetic samples.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two perfumers' claims?$q$, $q$["Perfumer B's cited results directly contradict the claim made by Perfumer A.","Perfumer B's findings confirm exactly what Perfumer A argues.","Both perfumers agree that synthetic compounds cannot be distinguished from natural ones.","Perfumer A argues that natural extracts are always superior."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Perfumer A claims trained noses cannot tell synthetic from natural scents, while Perfumer B cites tests in which panels reliably did — a direct contradiction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Even as flood warnings poured in through the night, the broadcaster's voice remained ______, never wavering into panic despite the escalating danger.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["steady","frantic","hesitant","calm"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Never wavering' emphasizes consistency sustained over the whole night, which 'steady' names directly; 'calm' describes a lack of distress at a given moment but doesn't by itself imply the voice held constant despite escalating danger.$q$),
  ($q$cs-59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A science article begins by asserting that life cannot exist without sunlight, then describes the discovery of thriving ecosystems around deep-sea hydrothermal vents that rely on chemical energy instead, using the vents to overturn the assumption stated at the outset.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the description of the deep-sea vents in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a counterexample that overturns the assumption presented in the opening sentence.","It confirms the assumption presented in the opening sentence.","It qualifies the opening claim as a rare exception rather than truly overturning it.","It summarizes the article's conclusion before any evidence is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage states directly that the vents are used 'to overturn the assumption stated at the outset,' not merely to carve out a minor exception to it, making choice A the accurate description of their function.$q$),
  ($q$cs-60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Linguist A argues that an endangered language is best revitalized through immersion schools, where children are taught primarily in that language from an early age.

Text 2: Linguist B argues that mobile apps and self-directed digital tools are more effective for revitalization today, since they can reach far more learners than a small number of physical schools can.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two linguists' approaches?$q$, $q$["Linguist A favors intensive, in-person immersion, while Linguist B favors scalable digital tools.","Both linguists agree that digital tools are ineffective for language learning.","Linguist B argues that immersion schools reach more learners than apps do.","Linguist A argues against teaching endangered languages to children."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Linguist A recommends in-person immersion schools, while Linguist B recommends digital tools that reach more learners — the contrast captured in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The restorer said the antique book's binding was surprisingly ______, still holding every page firmly in place after two centuries of handling.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["durable","fragile","loose","damaged"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A binding that still holds every page firmly in place after two centuries of handling is durable, not fragile, loose, or damaged.$q$),
  ($q$cs-62$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The tour guide's commentary was refreshingly ______, translating technical architectural terms into language any visitor could follow.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["accessible","obscure","technical","silent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Translating technical terms so 'any visitor could follow' describes accessible commentary, not obscure or overly technical language.$q$),
  ($q$cs-63$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Far from being ______, the intern's questions addressed genuine gaps in the training manual that even veteran staff had overlooked.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["superficial","rehearsed","confidential","welcomed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Far from being ___' signals a contrast: questions that addressed real gaps overlooked by veterans were the opposite of superficial.$q$),
  ($q$cs-64$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The potter's glazing technique was remarkably ______, producing the same deep blue finish on every single piece she fired that year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["consistent","erratic","accidental","faded"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Producing 'the same deep blue finish on every single piece' describes a consistent technique, not an erratic or accidental one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-65$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The diplomat's remarks were carefully ______, acknowledging both sides' concerns without endorsing either nation's position.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["balanced","biased","confrontational","dismissive"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Acknowledging both sides without favoring either describes balanced remarks, the opposite of biased or confrontational ones.$q$),
  ($q$cs-66$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The novelist's later prose grew increasingly ______, trimming any sentence that did not directly advance the plot.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["spare","ornate","verbose","sentimental"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Trimming every sentence that doesn't advance the plot describes spare, economical prose, not ornate or verbose writing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-67$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The climate dataset was considered ______ by the review board, since three independent research teams had reached the identical conclusion using different methods.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["robust","flawed","preliminary","contested"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Three independent teams reaching the same conclusion through different methods is a hallmark of a robust, well-supported dataset.$q$),
  ($q$cs-68$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Critics found the orchestra's performance ______, complaining that the strings lagged noticeably behind the brass section throughout the symphony.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ragged","polished","restrained","triumphant"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Strings that 'lagged noticeably behind' the brass describes a ragged, poorly coordinated performance, not a polished one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-69$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The company's pivot away from hardware was so ______ that even senior engineers learned of the change only after it appeared in a press release.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["abrupt","gradual","anticipated","transparent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Engineers learning of the change only from a press release shows the pivot happened with little warning, or abruptly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-70$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The historian's account of the mutiny was ______, drawing on ship logs, court-martial transcripts, and sailors' private letters rarely consulted by earlier scholars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["exhaustive","speculative","brief","biased"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Drawing on multiple rarely used sources describes a thorough, or exhaustive, account, not a brief or speculative one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-71$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Despite three rounds of cross-examination, the witness's account remained ______, matching her original statement in every detail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unwavering","contradictory","evolving","incomplete"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An account that 'match[es] her original statement in every detail' despite repeated cross-examination is unwavering, not contradictory.$q$),
  ($q$cs-72$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The city's renovation plan was criticized as ______, promising to rebuild twelve bridges and four transit lines within a single fiscal year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unrealistic","modest","overdue","thorough"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Promising to rebuild twelve bridges and four transit lines in one year is an implausibly large scope, making 'unrealistic' the fitting criticism.$q$),
  ($q$cs-73$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The poet's later collection embraced an ______ style, abandoning not just metaphor and ornament but any trace of warmth, until only the bare image remained.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["austere","ornate","minimalist","sentimental"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Stripping away both ornament and warmth describes more than simplicity — it's a stark, severe economy that 'austere' captures; 'minimalist' would fit a pared-down style but doesn't necessarily carry austere's connotation of coldness.$q$),
  ($q$cs-74$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Shareholders dismissed the CEO's apology as ______, noting that it never once mentioned the specific harm caused by the recall.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["perfunctory","heartfelt","detailed","brief"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Never mentioning the specific harm caused suggests the apology was issued as a hollow formality, not merely that it was short — 'brief' would describe length, while 'perfunctory' captures the lack of genuine engagement shareholders objected to.$q$),
  ($q$cs-75$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The archaeologist's conclusions remained ______, since the fragment could be read as evidence for either of the two rival theories about the site's original purpose.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["equivocal","conclusive","unanimous","ambivalent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The fragment supporting either rival theory equally makes the conclusion open to two readings, which 'equivocal' names; 'ambivalent' describes a person's own mixed feelings, not evidence that cuts both ways.$q$),
  ($q$cs-76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Throughout the tense trial, the lead juror kept an ______ expression, giving neither attorney any hint of her eventual vote.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["impassive","animated","sympathetic","stoic"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clue concerns what her face revealed — 'impassive' names an expression that gives nothing away — while 'stoic' describes an attitude of enduring hardship without complaint, which doesn't necessarily describe a blank, unreadable expression.$q$),
  ($q$cs-77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The senator's testimony was widely called ______, since she answered every question directly even when the truthful answer clearly damaged her own case.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["candid","evasive","rehearsed","guarded"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Answering directly even when the truth is damaging describes candid testimony, the opposite of evasive or guarded testimony.$q$),
  ($q$cs-78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The architect's design for the library was praised as ______, since a single reading room could be reconfigured within hours into a lecture hall, a gallery, or a quiet study space.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["adaptable","rigid","ornamental","temporary"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A room reconfigurable into several different uses within hours describes an adaptable design, not a rigid or ornamental one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on desert ecosystems opens by describing a single cactus surviving a two-year drought, then broadens to explain how root adaptations across desert plant species allow such survival, and closes by discussing conservation efforts to protect desert habitats.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It begins with a specific case, moves to broader supporting evidence, and ends with practical recommendations.","It presents two competing hypotheses and dismisses one entirely.","It opens with a policy proposal and closes with an unrelated anecdote.","It compares two desert ecosystems without discussing conservation."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text moves from one cactus's survival (specific case) to root adaptations across species (broader evidence) to conservation efforts (proposed action), matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A profile of a chess champion opens with a description of her losing her first tournament match at age seven, then explains that the loss motivated years of disciplined practice that eventually led to her international title.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It connects an early setback described in the first sentence to the champion's later success.","It contradicts the claim made in the first sentence.","It introduces a completely unrelated childhood memory.","It states the profile's conclusion before any details are given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence explains how the early loss described in the first sentence motivated the practice that led to her later success, linking the two sentences.$q$),
  ($q$cs-81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on rooftop gardens begins by noting that many cities lose green space to development, then describes how several buildings have converted unused roof areas into small farms that supply nearby restaurants.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a concrete response to the problem introduced in the first sentence.","It disputes the claim that cities are losing green space.","It shifts to a discussion of a completely different urban issue.","It concludes the article without offering any supporting detail."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence directly answers the problem raised in the first sentence by describing rooftop farms as a concrete response.$q$),
  ($q$cs-82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on the Japanese tea ceremony opens with a step-by-step description of preparing a single bowl of matcha, then explains the philosophical concept of ichigo ichie, or the idea that each gathering is unrepeatable, and closes by reflecting on how this idea might apply to everyday encounters.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a concrete ritual to its underlying philosophy to a broader life lesson.","It presents two rival ceremonial traditions and argues for one over the other.","It opens with a philosophical claim and ends with a technical manual.","It compares tea ceremonies across several unrelated countries."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay's stated progression — ritual, then philosophy, then a life lesson — matches choice A exactly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$In an essay defending a controversial urban rezoning plan, the author spends the opening paragraph summarizing residents' objections in detail before spending the remaining paragraphs addressing each objection point by point.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Acknowledging opposing objections in detail before responding to strengthen the author's case","Ignoring residents' objections to avoid weakening the plan's support","Presenting only the author's opinion without referencing any objections","Avoiding a clear position so readers can form their own conclusions"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Summarizing objections in detail before responding to each one is a concession-and-rebuttal strategy, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A profile of an inventor opens with an account of him tinkering with a broken radio as a boy, unable to afford a replacement. Decades later, the profile closes with his company donating refurbished electronics to schools that cannot afford new equipment.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the closing paragraph functions in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It echoes the opening anecdote, linking his early resourcefulness to his later philanthropy.","It introduces a new criticism of his business practices.","It shifts focus away from electronics entirely.","It contradicts the account given in the opening paragraph."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The closing paragraph revisits the resourcefulness from the opening anecdote in the form of philanthropy, creating a deliberate echo, as choice A describes.$q$),
  ($q$cs-85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An article on soil health begins by describing a farmer's dramatically improved crop yields after switching to no-till methods, then presents research data on how tilling disrupts fungal networks that plants rely on for nutrients.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It supplies a scientific explanation for the improvement described in the first sentence.","It contradicts the farmer's reported results.","It introduces an unrelated agricultural practice.","It restates the first sentence without adding new information."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence explains the mechanism (fungal networks) behind the improved yields described in the first sentence, functioning as a scientific explanation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A retrospective essay first details a painter's early, celebrated landscapes, then spends its remaining pages examining a little-known series of abstract works she produced privately and never exhibited during her lifetime.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To draw attention to an overlooked body of work alongside the painter's celebrated pieces","To argue that the painter's early landscapes were her only significant achievement","To provide technical instructions for landscape painting","To dismiss the value of the painter's private, unexhibited works"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay covers celebrated early work, then draws attention to an overlooked private series, matching choice A's description of the purpose.$q$),
  ($q$cs-87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on a controversial court ruling opens by summarizing the judge's stated reasoning, then spends several paragraphs presenting the historical precedents the judge appears to have overlooked, without ever directly stating that the ruling was incorrect.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Implying a flaw in the ruling by presenting overlooked precedents rather than stating the criticism outright","Praising the ruling directly and providing precedents that support it","Ignoring all legal precedent in favor of personal opinion","Stating a firm conclusion before presenting any precedents"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Presenting overlooked precedents without directly stating the ruling was wrong is an implicit rather than explicit criticism, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay begins by asserting that a river's fish populations had been in permanent decline for a century, then describes recent survey data showing salmon returning in numbers not seen since before industrialization, using the data to challenge the essay's own opening claim.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the survey data in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a counterexample that undermines the assumption stated at the beginning of the essay.","It confirms the assumption stated at the beginning of the essay.","It qualifies the opening claim as true only for the first half of the century rather than overturning it.","It summarizes the essay's conclusion before any evidence is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage states the survey data is used to challenge the essay's own opening claim outright, not merely to narrow its timeframe, so it functions as a counterexample undermining that assumption, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The essay opens by describing the harsh reviews a composer's symphony received at its premiere, then spends the remainder of the piece tracing how the same symphony came to be regarded, decades later, as one of the defining works of its era.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To trace how a work's critical reputation shifted dramatically over time","To argue that the symphony was rightly dismissed by its earliest audiences","To argue that the symphony's harsh initial reviews permanently damaged its legacy","To defend the harsh reviews the symphony initially received"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay traces the symphony's reputation from harsh initial reviews to later acclaim as a defining work, not a permanently damaged legacy, matching choice A's description of a shift in reputation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Botanist A argues that a rare fern species is disappearing primarily because of habitat destruction from logging, citing measured declines that track logging permits issued in the region.

Text 2: Botanist B argues that an invasive slug species introduced accidentally is the primary cause, citing that fern declines began in areas without logging but with confirmed slug sightings.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two botanists' views?$q$, $q$["Botanist A points to habitat destruction, while Botanist B points to an invasive species.","Both botanists agree that logging is unrelated to the fern's decline.","Botanist B relies exclusively on logging permit records.","Botanist A argues that invasive slugs caused the decline."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Botanist A attributes the decline to habitat destruction from logging, while Botanist B attributes it to an invasive slug species — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Sleep researcher A argues that blue light from screens before bed is the primary cause of shortened sleep duration in teenagers, citing survey data linking screen time to later bedtimes.

Text 2: Sleep researcher B argues that shifting school start times, not screen use, is the primary driver, citing that sleep duration improved when a district delayed its start time without any change in reported screen habits.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two researchers' views?$q$, $q$["Researcher A emphasizes screen exposure, while Researcher B emphasizes school scheduling.","Both researchers agree that screen time has no effect on sleep.","Researcher B relies exclusively on survey data about screen habits.","Researcher A argues that school start times caused the sleep changes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Researcher A points to blue light exposure, while Researcher B points to school start times, matching the contrast described in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Urban planner A argues that a city's traffic congestion is caused mainly by rapid population growth outpacing road capacity.

Text 2: Urban planner B argues that congestion is caused mainly by poor intersection design, noting that traffic flows smoothly in nearby cities with similar population growth but better-designed intersections.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two planners' views?$q$, $q$["Planner A points to population growth, while Planner B points to intersection design.","Both planners agree that population growth has no effect on congestion.","Planner B relies exclusively on population statistics.","Planner A argues that intersection design is the primary cause."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Planner A attributes congestion to population growth, while Planner B attributes it to intersection design — the contrast captured in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: A theater critic argues that minimalist stage sets force audiences to engage their imagination more actively than elaborate sets do.

