  ($q$cs-95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Art historian A attributes a disputed painting to a well-known master, citing stylistic similarities in brushwork to the master's confirmed works.

Text 2: Art historian B disputes this attribution, citing pigment analysis showing the paint was manufactured after the master's death.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Art historian B most likely respond to Art historian A's argument?$q$, $q$["By pointing to the pigment analysis as evidence that the painting could not be the master's work","By agreeing that stylistic similarities settle the question of attribution","By arguing that pigment analysis is never a reliable dating method","By claiming the master lived much longer than historical records indicate"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Art historian B's pigment evidence dates the paint to after the master's death, directly countering A's stylistic attribution.$q$),
  ($q$cs-96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Psychologist A found that participants who multitasked during a work task completed it more slowly and made more errors than those who focused on one task.

