  ($q$cs-99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The volunteer firefighters responded with impressive ______, arriving at the scene less than four minutes after the alarm sounded.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["speed","hesitation","reluctance","confusion"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Arriving in under four minutes shows a fast response, which 'speed' names directly; the other choices describe the opposite reaction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The museum's new wing was designed to be ______, allowing wheelchair users to reach every gallery without assistance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["accessible","exclusive","temporary","crowded"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A design that lets wheelchair users 'reach every gallery without assistance' is accessible, not exclusive or crowded.$q$),
  ($q$cs-101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The baker's sourdough loaves were praised as ______, each one rising to the same impressive height morning after morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["consistent","inedible","shrinking","random"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Loaves that rise 'to the same impressive height morning after morning' describes consistent results, not random or shrinking ones.$q$),
  ($q$cs-102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Parents found the new scheduling app's interface ______, since even first-time users could book an appointment within a minute.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["intuitive","confusing","outdated","expensive"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Users booking an appointment 'within a minute' on their first try points to an intuitive interface, not a confusing one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The marathon runner's pace stayed ______ for the entire race, never speeding up or slowing down by more than a few seconds per mile.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["steady","erratic","sluggish","frantic"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A pace that never varies by more than a few seconds per mile is steady, the opposite of erratic or sluggish.$q$),
  ($q$cs-104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The committee's decision was ______, reached only after every single member had voted the same way.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unanimous","divided","postponed","secret"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A decision 'reached only after every single member had voted the same way' describes a unanimous outcome, not a divided one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The gardener's advice proved ______, since the tomatoes she recommended thrived in exactly the soil conditions she predicted.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["accurate","useless","outdated","vague"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Tomatoes thriving in exactly the predicted soil conditions shows the advice was accurate, not vague or outdated.$q$),
  ($q$cs-106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The toddler's drawing was wonderfully ______, covering the entire page with bright, overlapping scribbles.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["chaotic","minimal","faded","symmetrical"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Bright, overlapping scribbles covering the entire page describe a chaotic drawing, the opposite of minimal or symmetrical.$q$),
  ($q$cs-107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Economists called the quarterly report ______, since it included data that directly contradicted the department's own earlier projections.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["embarrassing","flattering","routine","classified"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A report containing data that 'contradicted the department's own earlier projections' would be embarrassing for that department, not routine or flattering.$q$),
  ($q$cs-108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The mayor's proposal was criticized as ______, offering specific numbers for only one of the five programs it claimed to fund.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["vague","detailed","illegal","popular"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Offering specific numbers for only one of five programs describes a vague proposal overall, not a detailed one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The telescope's new sensor proved remarkably ______, detecting light from galaxies too faint for any previous instrument to register.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["sensitive","dull","expensive","outdated"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Detecting light 'too faint for any previous instrument to register' describes a highly sensitive sensor, not a dull one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Critics described the sculptor's latest work as ______, since it used the exact same bronze-casting method she had employed for the past twenty years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["stagnant","innovative","abstract","collaborative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using 'the exact same...method' for twenty years without change describes a stagnant body of work, the opposite of innovative.$q$),
  ($q$cs-111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The band's sound grew increasingly ______ over the decade, incorporating synthesizers, horn sections, and orchestral strings into songs that once featured only guitar and drums.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["elaborate","minimalist","quiet","repetitive"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding synthesizers, horns, and orchestral strings to once-simple arrangements describes an increasingly elaborate sound, not a minimalist one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The new treatment protocol was called ______ by hospital administrators, since it required no additional staff training or equipment purchases.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["cost-effective","experimental","risky","controversial"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A protocol that 'required no additional staff training or equipment purchases' is cost-effective from an administrator's perspective, not risky or experimental.$q$),
  ($q$cs-113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The judge's ruling was widely considered ______, since it departed sharply from decades of established precedent on the same question.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unprecedented","routine","expected","narrow"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A ruling that 'departed sharply from decades of established precedent' is unprecedented, not routine or expected.$q$),
  ($q$cs-114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Participants in the study reported feeling ______ after only ten minutes of the guided breathing exercise, with measured heart rates dropping noticeably.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["calmer","anxious","energized","distracted"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Heart rates 'dropping noticeably' after a breathing exercise indicates participants felt calmer, not anxious or energized.$q$),
  ($q$cs-115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The guidebook described the mountain trail as ______, warning hikers that a single wrong turn in dense fog could leave them miles off course.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["treacherous","straightforward","scenic","crowded"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A warning that 'a single wrong turn...could leave [hikers] miles off course' describes a treacherous trail, not a straightforward one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The renovated train station was praised as ______, preserving the original nineteenth-century facade while housing entirely new, modern platforms inside.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["harmonious","incongruous","dilapidated","temporary"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Preserving the old facade while adding modern platforms inside describes a harmonious blend of old and new, not an incongruous one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The novel's structure is ______, jumping between five different narrators' perspectives without warning from chapter to chapter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fragmented","linear","predictable","simple"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Jumping between five narrators 'without warning' describes a fragmented structure, the opposite of linear or simple.$q$),
  ($q$cs-118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Historians now consider the treaty's effects ______, since it simultaneously ended one conflict and planted the seeds for another decades later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["paradoxical","straightforward","negligible","beneficial"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A treaty that both 'ended one conflict' and 'planted the seeds for another' has contradictory effects, which 'paradoxical' captures.$q$),
  ($q$cs-119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The survey results were ______, since responses from urban and rural participants differed by less than two percentage points on every question.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["uniform","polarized","inconclusive","biased"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Responses differing 'by less than two percentage points' across groups describes uniform results, not polarized ones.$q$),
  ($q$cs-120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The philosopher's argument was ______, resting on a single assumption that, if false, would cause the entire conclusion to collapse.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["precarious","airtight","fragile","circular"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$An argument that would 'collapse' if one assumption failed is precariously balanced on that single point; 'fragile' suggests general delicacy but doesn't capture the specific dependency on one assumption the way 'precarious' does.$q$),
  ($q$cs-121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The researchers' claim was ______, supported by only a single small-scale trial that other labs had not yet attempted to replicate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["tentative","definitive","preliminary","widely confirmed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The claim's weakness is its lack of replication, making it cautiously held, or tentative; 'preliminary' would describe its place early in a research timeline, but a finding can be preliminary and still stated with confidence, so it doesn't capture the hedging the unreplicated trial demands.$q$),
  ($q$cs-122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The critic argued that the author's prose, for all its surface beauty, was fundamentally ______, since the ornate sentences rarely advanced the plot or revealed character.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["superfluous","essential","concise","decorative"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The critic's complaint is that the ornate prose serves no narrative function — superfluous means wholly unnecessary to the work's substance — while 'decorative' would concede the prose serves an aesthetic purpose, which the critic denies by saying it never advances plot or character.$q$),
  ($q$cs-123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The contract's clause was deliberately ______, allowing either party to interpret the obligation differently should a dispute arise.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ambiguous","explicit","vague","standard"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause supports two distinct, competing readings by design, which 'ambiguous' captures precisely; 'vague' would mean merely imprecise or unclear, not necessarily allowing two parties to construct different coherent interpretations.$q$),
  ($q$cs-124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The senator's proposal was ______ in its scope, addressing tax policy, healthcare, education funding, and immigration within a single bill.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["omnibus","narrow","modest","comprehensive"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clue is not just thoroughness but bundling unrelated subjects — tax, healthcare, education, immigration — into one bill, which 'omnibus' names specifically; 'comprehensive' could describe exhaustive coverage of a single topic without that multi-subject bundling.$q$),
  ($q$cs-125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The curator defended the exhibit's unconventional pairing of medieval tapestries with video art as ______, arguing the juxtaposition revealed unexpected continuities between eras.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inspired","arbitrary","offensive","unconventional"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The curator isn't just noting the pairing was unusual — she's defending its insight, that it 'revealed unexpected continuities'; 'unconventional' would only describe its novelty, not the justified creative vision 'inspired' conveys.$q$),
  ($q$cs-126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Analysts described the central bank's messaging as ______, since each official statement seemed to hint at a different timeline for interest rate changes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inconsistent","transparent","unanimous","nuanced"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Analysts' complaint is that the statements didn't line up with each other, suggesting no stable position — 'inconsistent' — rather than that each statement offered a carefully calibrated, more complex view, which 'nuanced' would suggest without implying contradiction.$q$),
  ($q$cs-127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on urban beekeeping opens by describing a rooftop hive producing jars of honey in its first season, then explains the specific flowering plants in the surrounding neighborhood that made the yield possible.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains a specific cause behind the result described in the first sentence.","It contradicts the claim made in the first sentence.","It introduces an unrelated hobby.","It states the article's conclusion before any evidence is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence explains the specific flowering plants that caused the strong honey yield described in the first sentence, functioning as a causal explanation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A short piece on community gardens begins by noting that empty lots often attract litter and neglect, then describes how one neighborhood converted such a lot into a thriving vegetable garden tended by residents.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a concrete solution to the problem raised in the first sentence.","It disputes that empty lots attract litter.","It shifts to a discussion of a different city issue.","It concludes the piece without offering any supporting detail."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence directly answers the problem of neglected lots raised in the first sentence by describing a thriving garden as a concrete response.$q$),
  ($q$cs-129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on a local bakery opens with a description of the aroma of fresh bread filling the street each morning, then broadens to profile the baker's decades-long apprenticeship, and closes with a note about her plans to open a second location.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a vivid sensory detail to background history to a glimpse of future plans.","It presents two competing bakeries and compares their products.","It opens with future plans and works backward to her childhood.","It argues that one bakery is superior to all others in the city."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The article's stated progression — aroma, then history, then future plans — matches choice A exactly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A nature documentary script opens by showing a single monarch butterfly departing on its migration, then widens to explain the thousands of miles the species travels as a whole, and ends by noting the conservation threats facing the species.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It begins with a specific example, broadens to general information, and ends with a concern.","It presents two rival theories about butterfly migration.","It opens with a policy proposal and ends with an unrelated anecdote.","It compares monarch butterflies to a different insect species throughout."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text moves from one butterfly's departure (specific example) to the species' migration as a whole (general information) to conservation threats (a concern), matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An essay on a small-town library begins by describing its crowded, outdated reading room, then explains that a recent grant allowed the library to double its space and add a children's section.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents a change that resolves the problem introduced in the first sentence.","It contradicts the description given in the first sentence.","It introduces an unrelated library in a different town.","It proves that the library never needed more space."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence describes a grant that doubled the library's space, directly resolving the crowding problem introduced in the first sentence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A profile of a long-distance swimmer opens with an account of her failing to finish her first open-water race, then explains that the setback led her to hire a coach who redesigned her training from the ground up.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It connects an early setback described in the first sentence to a change in her approach.","It contradicts the claim made in the first sentence.","It introduces a completely unrelated competition.","It states the profile's conclusion before any details are given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence explains how the failure described in the first sentence led her to hire a coach and change her training, linking the two sentences.$q$),
  ($q$cs-133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on the history of umbrellas opens by noting that early umbrellas were used almost exclusively by women as a fashion accessory, then argues that only after the 1850s did they become common, gender-neutral protection against rain, tracing the shift through period advertisements.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To trace a shift in how umbrellas were used and perceived over time","To argue that umbrellas should only be used as fashion accessories","To provide a technical manual for manufacturing umbrellas","To praise a single advertisement from the 1850s"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay opens with umbrellas' original use, then traces a shift toward common, gender-neutral use after the 1850s, matching choice A's description of the purpose.$q$),
  ($q$cs-134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A report on a local theater's renovation opens with a description of the leaking roof that forced its closure, presents in its middle section the fundraising campaign that paid for repairs, and closes with the sold-out reopening night.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the middle section of the report?$q$, $q$["To explain how the problem described in the opening was ultimately resolved","To contradict the claims made in the report's opening","To define technical terms related to theater architecture","To criticize the donors who funded the renovation"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The middle section's fundraising campaign explains how the roof problem from the opening was resolved, leading to the reopening described at the end.$q$),
  ($q$cs-135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on handwriting instruction opens by acknowledging that most schools have phased out cursive lessons, then spends its remaining paragraphs arguing that cursive still offers unique benefits for fine motor development that typing cannot replicate.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Acknowledging a trend before arguing against abandoning the practice entirely","Ignoring the decline of cursive instruction to focus solely on typing","Presenting only anecdotal evidence without any structured argument","Avoiding any clear position on whether cursive should be taught"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Acknowledging the decline of cursive instruction before arguing for its unique benefits is a concede-then-argue strategy, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An article on coral reefs opens with a diver's firsthand account of a vibrant, colorful reef, then presents data showing that nearby reefs without the same protections have lost most of their coral cover.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It supplies comparative evidence that highlights the value of the protections mentioned in the first sentence.","It contradicts the diver's firsthand account.","It introduces an unrelated marine species.","It concludes the article before any data has been presented."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence contrasts unprotected reefs with the protected one in the first sentence, supplying comparative evidence for the value of protection.$q$),
  ($q$cs-137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A biography of a chess-playing computer program spends its early chapters on the engineers' repeated failures to beat human champions, then devotes a full chapter to the single match in which the program finally succeeded, treating it as the culmination of the project.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the chapter devoted to that single match?$q$, $q$["It highlights a pivotal success that the biography treats as the project's turning point.","It disproves the claims made earlier about the engineers' failures.","It shifts entirely away from chess to focus on unrelated technology.","It summarizes the whole project before any details are given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text explicitly frames the single winning match as 'the culmination of the project,' so the chapter functions to highlight that pivotal success.$q$),
  ($q$cs-138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on lighthouse keepers opens by romanticizing the solitude of the job, then spends its final section describing the extreme isolation that drove many keepers to request transfers after only a single winter.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the final section in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It complicates the earlier romanticized view by introducing a harsher reality of the job.","It repeats the praise offered in the opening without adding anything new.","It shifts the topic entirely away from lighthouse keepers.","It proves that no lighthouse keeper ever found the job difficult."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After romanticizing the solitude of the job, the final section introduces the isolation that drove keepers to leave, complicating rather than repeating the earlier praise.$q$),
  ($q$cs-139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A museum essay on ancient pottery opens by describing the intricate geometric patterns visible on a surviving vase, then explains that similar patterns appear on textiles from the same region, suggesting a shared decorative tradition across crafts.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It extends the observation in the first sentence to suggest a broader cultural pattern.","It disputes the claim that the vase has geometric patterns.","It argues that pottery and textiles were never produced in the same region.","It shifts the essay's focus to an unrelated artifact with no connection to the vase."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence extends the pattern observed on the vase to textiles, suggesting a shared tradition across crafts rather than disputing or ignoring the first sentence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An article on competitive debate opens by describing a single student's halting, nervous first speech, then traces her development over four years of practice rounds and tournaments, and closes with her delivering a confident closing argument at the national championship.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It follows a chronological arc from early struggle to eventual mastery.","It presents two debaters and compares their styles side by side.","It opens with the national championship and works backward to her first speech.","It argues that nervousness is required for success in debate."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text follows a clear chronological progression from a nervous first speech to eventual confident mastery, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on a controversial dam project acknowledges the economic benefits its supporters cite, then spends several paragraphs detailing the ecological costs to downstream communities, without ever directly stating whether the dam should have been built.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Presenting both benefits and costs while leaving the overall judgment to the reader","Praising the dam directly without mentioning any drawbacks","Ignoring all economic considerations in favor of personal opinion","Stating a firm conclusion before presenting any evidence"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Presenting both the economic benefits and ecological costs without stating a final judgment leaves the overall assessment to the reader, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay opens by asserting that handwritten letters had become entirely obsolete by the early twenty-first century, then describes a resurgence of letter-writing clubs among young people who cite the format's slowness as its appeal, using this trend to challenge the essay's own opening claim.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the description of letter-writing clubs in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a counterexample that undermines the assumption presented in the opening sentence.","It confirms the assumption presented in the opening sentence.","It qualifies the opening claim as true only for business correspondence, not personal letters.","It summarizes the essay's conclusion before any evidence is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The passage states the trend is used 'to challenge the essay's own opening claim' directly, not to carve out a narrower exception for it, so the letter-writing clubs function as a counterexample undermining that assumption.$q$),
  ($q$cs-143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A history of a shipping canal devotes its opening section to the engineering challenges of its construction, its middle section to the political disputes between the nations that funded it, and its final section to the economic transformation it brought to global trade routes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It examines a single subject through three distinct lenses: technical, political, and economic.","It presents a single case study and dismisses its importance entirely.","It begins with economic transformation and works backward to construction.","It argues that the canal's construction was not politically significant."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The three sections examine the canal through engineering, political, and economic lenses in turn, matching choice A's description of multiple perspectives on one subject.$q$),
  ($q$cs-144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay begins by praising a factory town's rapid economic growth in the early twentieth century, then spends its final paragraphs detailing the long-term health consequences residents faced from industrial pollution that the growth figures never captured.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the final paragraphs in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["They complicate the earlier praise by revealing a cost the growth figures obscured.","They repeat the praise offered in the opening paragraphs without adding anything new.","They shift the focus to a neighboring factory town that avoided industrial pollution.","They prove that the factory town experienced no economic growth at all."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After praising economic growth, the final paragraphs reveal a health cost in the same town that the growth figures missed, complicating rather than repeating the earlier praise or shifting to a different town altogether.$q$),
  ($q$cs-145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on a philosopher's reputation opens by summarizing the harsh dismissal her work received from contemporaries, then spends the remainder of the piece tracing how later scholars, working decades after her death, came to regard her as a founder of an entirely new field.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To trace how a thinker's critical reputation shifted dramatically after her death","To argue that her contemporaries were correct to dismiss her work","To argue that her contemporaries' harsh dismissal permanently ended her influence","To defend the harsh dismissal she initially received"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay traces the philosopher's reputation from harsh contemporary dismissal to later recognition as a founder of a new field, not a permanently ended influence, matching choice A's description of a reputational shift.$q$),
  ($q$cs-146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Exercise scientist A argues that morning workouts burn more fat than evening workouts, citing a study showing higher fat oxidation rates in participants who exercised before breakfast.

Text 2: Exercise scientist B argues that workout timing makes no meaningful difference to fat loss over time, citing a longer study in which total body composition changes were nearly identical between morning and evening exercisers.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two scientists' views?$q$, $q$["Scientist A emphasizes short-term fat oxidation, while Scientist B emphasizes long-term body composition outcomes.","Both scientists agree that workout timing has no effect on fat loss.","Scientist B relies exclusively on short-term fat oxidation data.","Scientist A argues that evening workouts are more effective than morning workouts."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Scientist A's evidence concerns short-term fat oxidation, while Scientist B's longer study measured overall body composition change, the contrast described in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Animal trainer A argues that reward-based methods alone are sufficient to correct most dog behavior problems, citing faster progress in dogs trained only with treats and praise.

Text 2: Animal trainer B argues that reward-based methods work well for simple commands but are insufficient for serious aggression issues, which require a structured program combining rewards with clear boundaries.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two trainers' views?$q$, $q$["Trainer A sees reward-based methods as generally sufficient, while Trainer B sees them as insufficient for serious cases.","Both trainers agree that rewards should never be used in dog training.","Trainer B relies exclusively on treats and praise for aggression issues.","Trainer A argues that aggression issues require a different program entirely."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Trainer A sees reward-based methods as broadly sufficient, while Trainer B sees them as inadequate for serious aggression cases requiring added structure — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Coffee roaster A recommends grinding beans immediately before brewing, arguing that pre-ground coffee loses aromatic compounds within minutes of grinding.

Text 2: Coffee roaster B recommends buying pre-ground coffee for most home drinkers, arguing that the convenience outweighs a flavor difference most casual drinkers cannot detect.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two roasters' recommendations?$q$, $q$["Roaster B prioritizes convenience over the flavor concern that Roaster A considers most important.","Both roasters agree that pre-ground coffee should never be purchased.","Roaster A recommends pre-ground coffee for most home drinkers.","Roaster B argues that grinding immediately before brewing is essential."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Roaster B explicitly values convenience over the flavor loss Roaster A emphasizes, matching the trade-off described in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Education researcher A argues that spacing study sessions across several days produces better long-term retention than cramming the same material into one long session.

Text 2: Education researcher B argues that for simple memorization tasks, a single intensive session can produce retention nearly as strong as spaced sessions, though the advantage of spacing grows with more complex material.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two researchers' findings?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's findings partly qualify Researcher A's claim, suggesting the advantage of spacing depends on task complexity.","Researcher B's findings directly contradict Researcher A's claim in every case.","Both researchers studied only complex memorization tasks.","Researcher A found that cramming always works better than spacing."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Researcher B's finding that cramming works nearly as well for simple tasks qualifies, rather than flatly contradicts, Researcher A's general claim about spacing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Workplace researcher A argues that employees working from home report higher productivity, citing self-reported output surveys showing increased task completion.

Text 2: Workplace researcher B argues that self-reported surveys overstate productivity gains, citing time-tracking software data showing many remote employees take longer breaks than survey responses suggest.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two researchers' claims?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's data challenges the reliability of the method Researcher A relies on.","Researcher B's findings confirm exactly what Researcher A argues.","Both researchers used identical time-tracking methods.","Researcher A argues that self-reported surveys are unreliable."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Researcher B's time-tracking data questions the accuracy of the self-reported surveys Researcher A relies on, challenging that method's reliability.$q$),
  ($q$cs-151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Paleontologist A argues that a species of small dinosaur hunted in coordinated packs, citing trackway fossils showing multiple individuals moving together at matched speeds.

Text 2: Paleontologist B argues that the same trackways more likely reflect individuals migrating along the same route at different times, since the tracks show no evidence of the animals interacting with one another.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two paleontologists' interpretations?$q$, $q$["Paleontologist A interprets the trackways as evidence of coordinated group behavior, while Paleontologist B interprets them as unrelated individual movement.","Both paleontologists agree the trackways show no evidence of movement.","Paleontologist B argues the dinosaurs hunted in coordinated packs.","Paleontologist A relies exclusively on evidence of interaction between individuals."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Paleontologist A reads the trackways as coordinated group behavior, while Paleontologist B reads the same evidence as unrelated individual movement — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Energy analyst A argues that large-scale battery storage is the most practical way to stabilize a power grid that relies heavily on solar and wind.

Text 2: Energy analyst B argues that pumped hydro storage remains more practical at large scale than batteries, since it can store far more energy per dollar despite requiring specific geography.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two analysts' views?$q$, $q$["Analyst A favors battery storage, while Analyst B favors pumped hydro storage.","Both analysts agree that grid storage is unnecessary.","Analyst B argues that batteries are the cheaper option at large scale.","Analyst A argues that pumped hydro requires no specific geography."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Analyst A favors batteries, while Analyst B favors pumped hydro for its cost-per-energy advantage at scale — the contrast captured in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Archaeologist A argues that an ancient coastal population relied primarily on fish for protein, citing isotope analysis of bone collagen consistent with a marine diet.

Text 2: Archaeologist B argues that the same population relied mainly on hunted land animals, citing tool assemblages dominated by projectile points typically used for hunting large game.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two archaeologists' views?$q$, $q$["Archaeologist A points to isotope evidence for a marine diet, while Archaeologist B points to tool evidence for a land-based diet.","Both archaeologists agree the population ate no fish or land animals.","Archaeologist B relies exclusively on isotope analysis.","Archaeologist A argues the population hunted large land animals."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Archaeologist A cites isotope evidence for a marine diet, while Archaeologist B cites tool evidence for a land-based diet — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Marketing researcher A argues that humorous advertisements are more memorable than serious ones, citing recall tests in which viewers remembered humorous ads days later.

Text 2: Marketing researcher B argues that humorous ads are memorable but less persuasive, citing purchase-intent surveys showing serious ads more often convinced viewers to buy the product.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two researchers' findings?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's findings complicate Researcher A's by distinguishing memorability from persuasiveness.","Researcher B's findings exactly replicate Researcher A's results.","Both researchers found that serious ads are more memorable.","Researcher A found that humorous ads are less persuasive than serious ones."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Researcher B's distinction between memorability and persuasiveness adds nuance to, rather than simply repeats, Researcher A's claim about humorous ads.$q$),
  ($q$cs-155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: City planner A argues that planting fast-growing tree species is the best way to quickly expand urban shade coverage, citing their rapid canopy development within a few years.

Text 2: City planner B argues that slower-growing native species are the better long-term investment, citing their greater resistance to local pests and storms despite taking decades to mature.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two planners' recommendations?$q$, $q$["Planner A prioritizes rapid canopy growth, while Planner B prioritizes long-term resilience.","Both planners agree that native species grow faster than fast-growing species.","Planner B recommends fast-growing species for quick shade coverage.","Planner A argues that native species are more resistant to pests."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Planner A values quick canopy growth, while Planner B values long-term resilience despite slower growth — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-156$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Historian A argues that a city's low death toll during a historical epidemic resulted mainly from an early quarantine policy enacted by local officials.

Text 2: Historian B argues that the low death toll is better explained by the city's unusually young population at the time, noting that quarantine policies in nearby cities with similar measures did not produce the same results.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two historians' views?$q$, $q$["Historian A credits a policy intervention, while Historian B credits a demographic factor.","Both historians agree that quarantine policies were ineffective everywhere.","Historian B relies exclusively on quarantine policy records.","Historian A argues that the city's population was unusually young."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Historian A credits the quarantine policy, while Historian B credits the city's young population and notes that similar policies elsewhere didn't replicate the result — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-157$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Linguist A argues that a regional dialect's distinctive vowel shift spread through face-to-face contact in workplaces, citing its concentration among workers in a single industry.

Text 2: Linguist B argues that the same vowel shift spread primarily through regional radio broadcasts, citing that listeners far from the industry in question adopted the shift at a similar rate to workers themselves.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two linguists' explanations?$q$, $q$["Linguist A attributes the spread to direct workplace contact, while Linguist B attributes it to broadcast media exposure.","Both linguists agree the vowel shift did not spread beyond a single industry.","Linguist B relies exclusively on workplace contact data.","Linguist A argues that radio broadcasts caused the vowel shift."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Linguist A attributes the spread to workplace contact, while Linguist B attributes it to radio broadcasts reaching listeners outside that industry — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-158$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Art market analyst A argues that a sudden rise in a sculptor's auction prices reflects growing genuine appreciation for her work among collectors.

Text 2: Art market analyst B argues that the price rise more likely reflects speculative buying by investors seeking short-term profit, citing that resale listings appeared within months of the original purchases, far sooner than typical collector behavior.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Art market analyst B most likely respond to Art market analyst A's claim?$q$, $q$["By pointing to the rapid resale pattern as evidence of speculation rather than genuine appreciation","By agreeing that the price rise reflects only genuine collector appreciation","By arguing that resale timing is irrelevant to understanding art prices","By claiming that the sculptor's prices never actually increased"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Analyst B's evidence of rapid resale timing supports a speculation-driven explanation rather than Analyst A's genuine-appreciation account.$q$),
  ($q$cs-159$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Cognitive scientist A argues that experienced chess players recognize board patterns through rapid, intuitive pattern matching built from years of practice, largely bypassing step-by-step calculation.

Text 2: Cognitive scientist B argues that eye-tracking data complicates this view, since expert players' gaze patterns show extensive, rapid calculation of multiple move sequences even when their final choice appears instantaneous.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two scientists' claims?$q$, $q$["Scientist B's evidence complicates Scientist A's claim by suggesting rapid calculation occurs even in seemingly intuitive decisions.","Scientist B's findings confirm exactly what Scientist A argues.","Both scientists agree that calculation plays no role in expert chess decisions.","Scientist A argues that eye-tracking data is irrelevant to chess expertise."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Scientist B's eye-tracking evidence suggests rapid calculation underlies even seemingly intuitive expert decisions, complicating Scientist A's purely intuitive account.$q$),
  ($q$cs-160$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Anthropologist A argues that strikingly similar flood myths across unconnected ancient cultures suggest a shared memory of a single, real prehistoric flood event.

Text 2: Anthropologist B argues that such similarities more likely arise independently in any culture near rivers or coastlines, since flooding is a common hazard that would naturally inspire comparable stories without any shared origin.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Anthropologist B most likely respond to Anthropologist A's claim?$q$, $q$["By arguing that similar environmental hazards, not a single shared event, better explain the parallel myths","By agreeing that a single prehistoric flood explains all the myths","By claiming that flood myths appear only in cultures far from water","By stating that the myths in question are not actually similar"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Anthropologist B's environmental-hazard explanation directly counters Anthropologist A's claim of a single shared flood event.$q$),
  ($q$cs-161$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$After the hive's queen died, the colony grew ______, with worker bees abandoning the orderly rotation of tasks they had followed for months.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disorganized","orderly","productive","calm"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Workers 'abandoning the orderly rotation of tasks' after the queen's death describes a disorganized colony, not an orderly or calm one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-162$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The chess engine's opening move stunned grandmasters, who called it ______ since no recorded game in over a century of tournament play had ever attempted it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unprecedented","routine","cautious","popular"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A move 'no recorded game...had ever attempted' is by definition unprecedented, not routine or popular.$q$),
  ($q$cs-163$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The librarian's cataloging method was praised as ______, with every manuscript labeled, cross-referenced, and shelved according to a strict, unbroken system.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["meticulous","careless","hurried","informal"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Labeling and cross-referencing 'every manuscript...according to a strict, unbroken system' describes meticulous work, not a careless or informal method.$q$),
  ($q$cs-164$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Below the dam's open floodgates, the once-placid river turned ______, churning with whitewater that capsized two kayaks within minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["turbulent","tranquil","frozen","shallow"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A river 'churning with whitewater' that capsizes kayaks is turbulent, the opposite of the 'once-placid' state it is contrasted with.$q$),
  ($q$cs-165$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The comedian's timing was ______: every pause landed exactly when the audience expected a punchline, never a beat too early or too late.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["impeccable","erratic","awkward","accidental"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Pauses that land 'exactly' right, 'never a beat too early or too late,' describe impeccable timing, not erratic or awkward timing.$q$),
  ($q$cs-166$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Thanks to a mild summer and steady rain, this year's grape harvest was unusually ______, filling every storage bin the vineyard owned.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["bountiful","meager","delayed","spoiled"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A harvest that fills 'every storage bin the vineyard owned' is a large, or bountiful, harvest, not a meager or spoiled one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-167$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The company's software update rolled out ______, with millions of users switching to the new interface without a single reported crash.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["seamlessly","disastrously","slowly","partially"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A rollout to millions of users 'without a single reported crash' is a seamless rollout, not a disastrous or partial one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-168$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$To avoid an avalanche-prone slope, the guide chose a ______ route that added three extra hours to what should have been a short climb.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["circuitous","direct","steep","forbidden"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A route chosen specifically to avoid danger that 'added three extra hours' to a short climb is an indirect, or circuitous, route, not a direct one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-169$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Facing a revenue shortfall, the city council proposed an ______ budget that eliminated nearly every discretionary program from the previous year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["austere","generous","flexible","secret"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A budget that 'eliminated nearly every discretionary program' in response to a shortfall is austere, not generous or flexible.$q$),
  ($q$cs-170$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The illustrator's style was undeniably ______, filling each page with floating teacups, grinning clouds, and impossible staircases.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["whimsical","somber","realistic","minimalist"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Floating teacups, grinning clouds, and impossible staircases describe a playful, whimsical style, not a somber or realistic one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-171$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$After the redesign, the assembly line became far more ______, cutting the number of steps needed to build each unit from twelve to four.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["streamlined","cluttered","outdated","manual"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cutting the number of steps 'from twelve to four' describes a streamlined process, not a cluttered or outdated one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-172$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Knowing the jury's attention would wander, the attorney kept her opening statement ______, covering the case's key facts in under three minutes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["succinct","rambling","vague","lengthy"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Covering the key facts 'in under three minutes' describes a succinct statement, the opposite of rambling or lengthy.$q$),
  ($q$cs-173$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Critics called the new concert hall's acoustics ______, noting that even a whisper from the stage carried clearly to the farthest row without any amplification.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["resonant","muffled","echoing","deserted"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Carrying a whisper 'clearly' to the back row describes rich, clear acoustics — resonant — not a hall with excessive echo, where multiple reflections would blur rather than clarify a faint sound.$q$),
  ($q$cs-174$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The startup's decision to freeze all hiring came so ______ that recruiters who had extended offers the previous week had to rescind them overnight.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["precipitously","gradually","transparently","swiftly"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The jarring reversal — offers rescinded 'overnight' — signals a sudden, reckless change in course, which 'precipitously' conveys; 'swiftly' only describes speed and could just as easily describe an efficient, well-managed decision, not one that blindsided recruiters.$q$),
  ($q$cs-175$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The marathoner's pace was ______, never slowing through brutal heat or a punishing final hill, until she crossed the finish line exactly on her predicted time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["relentless","erratic","leisurely","declining"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A pace that 'never' slows despite heat and a hill, finishing exactly on schedule, is relentless, not erratic or declining.$q$),
  ($q$cs-176$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Inside the case, the clock's mechanism was astonishingly ______, its hundreds of tiny brass gears meshing to track not only the hour but the phase of the moon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["intricate","simple","rusted","elaborate"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clue is functional complexity — gears 'meshing' to track multiple things at once — which 'intricate' names; 'elaborate' suggests ornateness or decorative detail, not necessarily the interlocking mechanical complexity the gears display.$q$),
  ($q$cs-177$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Wary of leaks, the ambassador kept her cable reports deliberately ______, omitting any detail that might reveal the source of her information.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["guarded","candid","exhaustive","public"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Deliberately 'omitting any detail' that could expose a source describes guarded reports, the opposite of candid or exhaustive ones.$q$),
  ($q$cs-178$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The magician's final trick was executed so ______ that even the stagehands who had rehearsed it twenty times still couldn't spot the switch.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["flawlessly","clumsily","slowly","obviously"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A trick that fools stagehands who rehearsed it 'twenty times' was performed flawlessly, not clumsily or obviously.$q$),
  ($q$cs-179$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$After months of drilling, the geologist's core samples proved frustratingly ______, showing traces of three different minerals that could each explain the anomaly equally well.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inconclusive","definitive","obvious","ambiguous"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Three equally plausible explanations leave the investigation undecided — inconclusive describes evidence that fails to settle a question, while 'ambiguous' more naturally describes a statement with unclear meaning, not physical samples that simply don't resolve between hypotheses.$q$),
  ($q$cs-180$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The tailor's stitching was so ______ that not even a magnifying glass revealed where one seam ended and the next began.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["precise","sloppy","visible","uneven"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Stitching invisible 'even' under a magnifying glass is precise, not sloppy or uneven.$q$),
  ($q$cs-181$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$When the well ran dry, the farmer proved remarkably ______, rigging a system of rain barrels and gutters that kept the crops alive until the next storm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["resourceful","helpless","wasteful","indifferent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Rigging a makeshift irrigation system to save the crops describes resourcefulness, not helplessness or indifference.$q$),
  ($q$cs-182$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The editor's notes on the manuscript were ______, pinpointing the exact chapter where the plot lost momentum and explaining precisely why.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["incisive","vague","flattering","delayed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Notes that pinpoint 'the exact chapter' and explain the problem 'precisely' are incisive, not vague or flattering.$q$),
  ($q$cs-183$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The forecaster's tone turned ______ as the storm's projected path shifted directly toward the coastal towns.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["dire","casual","cheerful","bored"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A storm path shifting toward populated towns would prompt an urgent, or dire, tone, not a casual or cheerful one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-184$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Visitors complained that the new exhibit felt ______, since artifacts from three unrelated centuries were scattered across a single room with no connecting theme.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disjointed","cohesive","chronological","eclectic"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Visitors are complaining about a lack of connection, which 'disjointed' captures as a flaw; 'eclectic' describes a varied mix that could be a deliberate, even admired, curatorial choice rather than a complaint-worthy lack of theme.$q$),
  ($q$cs-185$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Facing backlash, the senator's apology was notably ______, admitting fault without a single qualifying excuse.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["contrite","defiant","sarcastic","vague"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Admitting fault 'without a single qualifying excuse' describes a contrite apology, not a defiant or vague one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-186$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Every night the saxophonist's solo was different, ______ enough that even her own bandmates never knew which notes would come next.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["inventive","rehearsed","identical","silent"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solos that differ nightly, surprising even her bandmates, are inventive, not rehearsed or identical.$q$),
  ($q$cs-187$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The shipping company's delivery record remained ______ throughout the holiday season, with not a single package arriving late despite record order volumes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["impeccable","spotty","delayed","average"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Not a single package arriving late' despite record volumes describes an impeccable record, not a spotty or average one.$q$),
  ($q$cs-188$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Assembling gears smaller than a grain of rice, the watchmaker's hands stayed remarkably ______, never once trembling under the magnifying lens.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["steady","shaky","hurried","nimble"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clue is about trembling, not speed or dexterity — 'steady' names the absence of tremor directly, while 'nimble' describes quick, agile movement that says nothing about whether the hands shake.$q$),
  ($q$cs-189$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An essay on watchmaking opens by describing the hundreds of tiny parts inside a single mechanical watch, then explains that digital watches nearly wiped out the trade by the 1980s, and closes by noting a small but growing number of apprentices now training in the craft.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a detailed description to a historical setback to a note of present-day revival.","It presents two competing theories and refutes one of them.","It opens with a conclusion and works backward to the evidence.","It compares watchmaking to an unrelated craft throughout the essay."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay's stated progression — mechanical detail, then the digital-watch setback, then apprentice revival — matches choice A exactly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-190$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Many people assume that sign languages are simply gestural translations of spoken language, with no grammar of their own. In fact, linguists have documented that sign languages have their own distinct grammatical structures, word orders, and even regional dialects, entirely independent of the spoken languages around them.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To complicate a popular assumption about the nature of sign languages","To argue that sign languages are inferior to spoken languages","To describe the physical hand shapes used in one specific sign language","To praise the inventor of a particular sign language"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text opens with a common assumption and then complicates it with linguistic evidence ('in fact...'), matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-191$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Air quality monitors in a city five hundred miles from the nearest wildfire recorded hazardous smoke levels for three straight days. Meteorologists explained that a persistent jet-stream pattern had carried the smoke plume directly over the region without dispersing it.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains the atmospheric cause behind the surprising statistic given in the first sentence.","It contradicts the measurement described in the first sentence.","It introduces an unrelated wildfire in a different country.","It proves that wildfire smoke never travels far from its source."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The jet-stream explanation accounts for how smoke reached a city 'five hundred miles' away, directly explaining the surprising statistic in the first sentence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-192$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A biography of a mathematician spends two chapters on the elegant theorems that made her famous early in her career, then devotes an entire chapter to a proof she attempted for twenty years and never completed, arguing that this prolonged failure shaped her later, more cautious approach to publishing new work.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the chapter on the uncompleted proof in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains how a prolonged failure influenced a meaningful change in her later approach to her work.","It proves that her early theorems were actually incorrect.","It shows that she abandoned mathematics entirely after the failed proof.","It summarizes her entire career before any chapters have been written."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text explicitly states the failed proof 'shaped her later, more cautious approach' to publishing, not that she left the field, matching choice A's description of the chapter's function.$q$),
  ($q$cs-193$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An essay on the Japanese tea ceremony opens by describing the precise, unhurried movements used to prepare a single bowl of tea, then explains how each gesture reflects a broader philosophy of mindfulness, and closes by reflecting on how the ceremony offers a rare pause in a fast-paced modern life.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a specific ritual description to its underlying philosophy to a broader reflection.","It presents two rival philosophies and evaluates each in turn.","It begins with a modern complaint and ends with an ancient ritual unrelated to it.","It compares the tea ceremony to an unrelated ritual from a different culture."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay's stated progression — ritual description, then philosophy, then reflection — matches choice A exactly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-194$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The op-ed acknowledges that solar subsidies cost taxpayers money in the short term, a point frequently raised by critics, before arguing in the following paragraphs that the subsidies pay for themselves within a decade through reduced healthcare costs linked to cleaner air.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Conceding a valid point raised by critics before arguing that long-term benefits outweigh it","Ignoring the financial cost of the subsidies entirely","Presenting only personal opinion without referencing any costs or benefits","Avoiding any position on whether the subsidies are worthwhile"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The op-ed explicitly concedes the critics' cost point before countering with long-term benefits, a concession-and-rebuttal strategy matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-195$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Many parents assume that limiting screen time is the only way to protect young children's attention spans. A recent study complicates this view, finding that the content children watch, not simply the number of hours, had a far stronger relationship with later attention difficulties.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To complicate a common assumption about what most affects children's attention spans","To argue that screen time has no effect on children whatsoever","To provide a detailed history of children's television programming","To recommend a specific brand of parental control software"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The text opens with a parental assumption and then complicates it with study findings about content mattering more than hours, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-196$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A report on the search for a sunken research vessel opens with a detailed account of the final hours before it disappeared, then uses the search itself to explain how sonar technology maps the ocean floor in total darkness.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the opening account in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a vivid, concrete narrative that anchors the technical explanation discussed later.","It proves that sonar technology cannot locate sunken vessels.","It argues against funding further searches for sunken ships.","It states the report's conclusion before any explanation is given."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using a vivid narrative to introduce and later illustrate a technical process is a common structural device, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-197$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$A local historian's essay first repeats the popular rumor that a century-old hotel is haunted because of a guest who vanished without a trace, then presents the hotel's original guest ledger, which shows the same guest checked out normally three days later under a slightly misspelled name.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the ledger evidence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It undermines the popular rumor described earlier in the essay with a documented record.","It confirms that the hotel is indeed haunted.","It introduces an unrelated rumor about a different hotel.","It restates the rumor without adding any new information."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The ledger shows the guest 'checked out normally,' directly undermining the vanishing-guest rumor presented earlier in the essay.$q$),
  ($q$cs-198$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A chef's memoir opens with a vivid memory of standing on a stool to stir a pot of her grandmother's soup, an experience she credits with sparking her love of cooking. The memoir closes decades later with her own grandchild standing on a stool beside her, stirring the same recipe, though the memoir never states the connection outright.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the closing scene functions in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It creates a deliberate parallel with the opening memory, implicitly linking her past to a new generation.","It introduces a critique of her grandmother's original recipe.","It shifts the memoir's focus away from cooking entirely.","It contradicts the memory described in the opening of the memoir."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The closing scene mirrors the opening image of a child stirring the same soup 'without stating it directly,' a structural echo linking generations, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-199$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on the zipper's history opens with its clumsy, unreliable nineteenth-century precursor, then describes the improved sliding-tooth design patented decades later, and ends with the garment industry's slow, reluctant adoption of the zipper over traditional buttons.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It traces the invention's development chronologically from an early flawed version to its eventual adoption.","It presents a single modern design and compares it to a rival design from the same year.","It begins with the zipper's widespread adoption and works backward to its invention.","It argues that buttons remain superior to zippers in every situation."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The three stages — flawed precursor, improved design, eventual adoption — move in chronological order, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-200$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A translator working on a newly unearthed set of cuneiform tablets initially assumed they recorded a royal decree, given their formal opening lines. Only after cross-referencing symbols with a separate archive did she realize the tablets were actually a merchant's detailed inventory of grain shipments.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It overturns the initial assumption stated in the first sentence with evidence from further research.","It confirms the initial assumption stated in the first sentence.","It suggests the tablets record both a royal decree and a merchant's inventory at once.","It summarizes the chapter's conclusion before any research has occurred."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cross-referencing symbols revealed the tablets were actually a grain inventory, not a royal decree — a full overturning of the first assumption, not a tablet that is somehow both at once.$q$),
  ($q$cs-201$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A museum report on a recovered shipwreck relic opens by describing the corroded bronze fragment as it looked when first pulled from the seafloor, then details the specific chemical process used to stabilize the metal, and closes by explaining what the restored fragment reveals about ancient trade routes.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a description of an object's condition to the technical process restoring it to its historical significance.","It presents two rival restoration techniques and argues that one is clearly inferior.","It opens with the historical significance and never returns to the object itself.","It compares the relic to an unrelated artifact recovered from a different wreck."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The report's stated progression — condition, then process, then historical significance — matches choice A exactly.$q$),
  ($q$cs-202$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An essay on a disappearing regional dialect opens by describing how it was spoken in nearly every household a century ago, then traces its gradual decline through consolidated schools that discouraged its use, and ends by describing a handful of elderly speakers now being recorded by linguists before the dialect disappears entirely.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It traces the dialect's decline chronologically from widespread use to its current, endangered state.","It presents two competing dialects and declares one superior.","It begins with the dialect's disappearance and works backward to explain its origin.","It argues that the dialect never actually existed as described."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The essay moves chronologically from widespread use, through decline, to the current endangered state, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-203$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$Competitive debate judges are often assumed to score rounds based solely on which side argued more persuasively. In practice, many scoring rubrics weight the clarity of a team's organization just as heavily as the persuasiveness of its individual arguments.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It complicates the assumption introduced in the first sentence with a more detailed account of actual practice.","It restates the assumption from the first sentence in different words.","It introduces an unrelated topic about debate team travel.","It proves that persuasiveness plays no role in debate scoring."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence shows that organization is weighted 'just as heavily' as persuasiveness, complicating the assumption that persuasiveness alone determines scores.$q$),
  ($q$cs-204$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The op-ed first presents the objection that stricter noise zoning would harm nightlife businesses already struggling to recover, then spends its remaining paragraphs citing cities where similar zoning increased, rather than decreased, evening foot traffic by making streets more pleasant to walk.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Raising an opposing concern directly in order to counter it with contrary evidence","Dismissing all concerns about nightlife businesses without addressing them","Relying solely on the author's personal opinion without any supporting data","Avoiding any clear position on noise zoning policy"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The op-ed explicitly raises the nightlife objection before countering it with evidence from other cities, a concession-and-rebuttal strategy matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-205$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A lighthouse keeper's memoir opens with his first night alone on the rock, watching for ships he feared he might fail to warn in time. Decades later, the memoir closes with his final night before retirement, during which a storm forces him to guide a struggling fishing boat safely past the rocks he once feared.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes how the closing scene functions in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It echoes the fear expressed in the opening, showing how his early uncertainty gave way to competence.","It introduces a new character who is never mentioned again.","It contradicts the memoir's opening description of his first night.","It shifts the memoir's focus entirely away from lighthouse keeping."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The closing storm scene revisits the fear from his 'first night' and shows him successfully guiding a boat, a structural echo that traces growth, matching choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-206$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 1, $q$An article on the umbrella's history begins by noting that early versions were used mainly as sunshades in ancient societies, then explains that it took centuries of waterproofing innovations before the umbrella became a practical tool against rain.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It explains how the umbrella's original purpose differed from the later use described in the same sentence.","It contradicts the claim that the umbrella was originally a sunshade.","It introduces an unrelated invention from a different century.","It proves that umbrellas have never been effective against rain."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second sentence traces how 'waterproofing innovations' transformed the umbrella from a sunshade into a rain tool, explaining the shift rather than contradicting the first sentence.$q$),
  ($q$cs-207$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$An article on wolf reintroduction opens with a single park ranger's account of spotting the first wild pup born in the region in seventy years, then presents population data showing elk herds beginning to disperse more naturally across the valley, and closes by recommending the approach be extended to two neighboring parks.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?$q$, $q$["It moves from a specific anecdote to supporting data to a policy recommendation.","It presents two competing policies and rejects both of them.","It presents the elk population data before the ranger's anecdote, then closes without a recommendation.","It compares wolf reintroduction to an unrelated conservation effort in a different country."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The article's stated progression — ranger's anecdote first, then elk data, then a policy recommendation — matches choice A exactly; the article never reverses that order or closes without a recommendation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-208$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Astronomer A argues that an exoplanet's habitability depends primarily on its distance from its star, placing it within a temperature range where liquid water could exist.

Text 2: Astronomer B argues that atmospheric composition matters more than orbital distance, noting that a planet in the ideal temperature zone could still be uninhabitable without an atmosphere capable of retaining heat and blocking radiation.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two astronomers' views?$q$, $q$["Astronomer A emphasizes orbital distance, while Astronomer B emphasizes atmospheric composition.","Both astronomers agree that orbital distance is irrelevant to habitability.","Astronomer B relies exclusively on temperature measurements.","Astronomer A argues that atmosphere is the most important factor."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Astronomer A points to orbital distance and temperature, while Astronomer B points to atmospheric composition — the contrast captured in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-209$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Horticulturist A recommends hot composting, turning the pile frequently to maintain high internal temperatures that kill weed seeds and pathogens within weeks.

Text 2: Horticulturist B recommends cold composting, arguing that the slower, untended method produces richer soil microbial life, even though it can take a year or more to finish.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two horticulturists' recommendations?$q$, $q$["Horticulturist A prioritizes speed and pathogen control, while Horticulturist B prioritizes microbial richness despite slower results.","Both horticulturists agree that cold composting kills pathogens faster.","Horticulturist B recommends frequent turning to raise internal temperature.","Horticulturist A argues that composting should take at least a year."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Horticulturist A values the speed and pathogen control of hot composting, while Horticulturist B values the microbial richness of the slower cold method — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-210$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Toxicologist A argues that current pesticide residue limits on produce adequately protect consumers, citing safety margins built into regulatory testing.

Text 2: Toxicologist B argues that current limits fail to account for cumulative exposure from eating multiple treated foods daily, and recommends stricter combined limits.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Toxicologist B most likely respond to Toxicologist A's claim?$q$, $q$["By arguing that the existing limits overlook cumulative exposure from multiple foods","By agreeing that current limits fully account for all consumer risk","By claiming that pesticide residue testing is unnecessary","By stating that cumulative exposure has no effect on safety"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Toxicologist B's stated position is that current limits 'fail to account for cumulative exposure,' directly countering A's claim that existing limits are adequate.$q$),
  ($q$cs-211$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Sociologist A argues that social media platforms increase civic participation by lowering the barriers to organizing protests and petitions.

Text 2: Sociologist B argues that the same platforms often produce only shallow, low-commitment forms of activism, noting survey data showing that most users who shared political posts took no further action afterward.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two sociologists' claims?$q$, $q$["Sociologist B's evidence complicates Sociologist A's claim by distinguishing easy online engagement from sustained civic action.","Sociologist B's findings confirm exactly what Sociologist A argues.","Both sociologists agree that social media has no effect on civic participation.","Sociologist A argues that most online activism involves no further action."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Sociologist B's survey data showing 'no further action' after sharing posts complicates Sociologist A's claim that lowered barriers translate into real civic participation.$q$),
  ($q$cs-212$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Entomologist A argues that firefly populations are declining mainly because of increased artificial light at night, which disrupts the flashing signals fireflies use to find mates.

Text 2: Entomologist B argues that habitat loss from development is the primary cause, noting that firefly populations have declined even in some areas where artificial light has not increased.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two entomologists' views?$q$, $q$["Entomologist A points to light pollution, while Entomologist B points to habitat loss.","Both entomologists agree that firefly populations are stable.","Entomologist B argues that artificial light is the primary cause of decline.","Entomologist A claims that habitat loss has no effect on fireflies."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Entomologist A attributes the decline to artificial light, while Entomologist B attributes it to habitat loss, citing declines where light has not increased — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-213$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Architect A argues that open floor plans in offices boost collaboration by removing physical barriers between employees.

Text 2: Architect B argues that open floor plans actually reduce productive collaboration, citing studies in which employees in open offices sent more messages electronically rather than speaking face-to-face, to preserve what little privacy they had.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two architects' claims?$q$, $q$["Architect B's cited evidence directly challenges the benefit Architect A attributes to open floor plans.","Architect B's findings confirm exactly what Architect A argues.","Both architects agree that open floor plans eliminate all workplace communication.","Architect A argues that employees prefer electronic messages to conversation."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Architect B cites evidence of reduced face-to-face communication in open offices, directly challenging Architect A's claim that open plans boost collaboration.$q$),
  ($q$cs-214$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Veterinarian A recommends commercially formulated pet food, arguing that it is nutritionally balanced according to standards tested across large populations of animals.

Text 2: Veterinarian B recommends raw diets tailored to an individual pet, arguing that commercial formulas use a one-size-fits-all approach that cannot account for a specific animal's needs.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two veterinarians' recommendations?$q$, $q$["Veterinarian A favors standardized, tested nutrition, while Veterinarian B favors individualized diets.","Both veterinarians agree that raw diets are nutritionally balanced for all pets.","Veterinarian B recommends commercial pet food over raw diets.","Veterinarian A argues that pets' needs vary too much for any standard diet."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Veterinarian A values standardized, population-tested nutrition, while Veterinarian B values diets tailored to the individual animal — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-215$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Meteorologist A argues that rapid hurricane intensification is driven primarily by unusually warm ocean surface temperatures that fuel storm energy.

Text 2: Meteorologist B argues that low atmospheric wind shear is the more decisive factor, noting storms over similarly warm waters that failed to intensify rapidly when wind shear remained high.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two meteorologists' explanations?$q$, $q$["Meteorologist A emphasizes ocean temperature, while Meteorologist B emphasizes atmospheric wind shear.","Both meteorologists agree that wind shear is irrelevant to intensification.","Meteorologist B relies exclusively on ocean temperature data.","Meteorologist A argues that wind shear is the decisive factor."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Meteorologist A points to warm ocean temperatures, while Meteorologist B points to low wind shear, citing storms over warm water that failed to intensify when shear was high — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-216$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Labor economist A argues that gig work expands economic opportunity by letting workers set flexible schedules unavailable in traditional jobs.

Text 2: Labor economist B argues that this flexibility comes at the cost of job security, pointing to survey data showing gig workers report far greater income volatility month to month than traditionally employed workers.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes the relationship between the two economists' claims?$q$, $q$["Economist B's data complicates Economist A's claim by weighing a cost against the flexibility it emphasizes.","Economist B's findings confirm exactly what Economist A argues.","Both economists agree that gig work provides no flexibility.","Economist A argues that gig workers have highly stable incomes."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Economist B's income-volatility data introduces a cost that complicates, rather than confirms, Economist A's purely positive framing of gig work's flexibility.$q$),
  ($q$cs-217$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Art conservator A recommends cleaning old paintings with traditional solvents, arguing that decades of documented use make the technique's effects predictable.

Text 2: Art conservator B recommends laser cleaning, arguing that it removes grime more precisely without the risk of solvents seeping into and damaging original paint layers.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two conservators' approaches?$q$, $q$["Conservator A favors a long-established method, while Conservator B favors a more precise, lower-risk technique.","Both conservators agree that solvents never damage paintings.","Conservator B recommends solvents over laser cleaning.","Conservator A argues that laser cleaning is more precise."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Conservator A values the predictability of a long-used solvent method, while Conservator B values the precision and lower risk of laser cleaning — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-218$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Immunologist A argues that booster shots should be given at fixed yearly intervals to maintain consistent population-wide immunity.

Text 2: Immunologist B argues that booster timing should instead be based on individual antibody levels, noting that fixed schedules give some people boosters too early and others too late.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two immunologists' recommendations?$q$, $q$["Immunologist A favors a uniform schedule, while Immunologist B favors individualized timing based on antibody levels.","Both immunologists agree that booster timing should never be measured.","Immunologist B recommends a fixed yearly schedule for all patients.","Immunologist A argues that antibody levels vary too much to measure."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Immunologist A recommends a uniform yearly schedule, while Immunologist B recommends timing based on individual antibody levels — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-219$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Urban historian A argues that a neighborhood's rapid gentrification was driven primarily by an influx of artists and small galleries that made the area culturally fashionable.

Text 2: Urban historian B argues that a new transit line, built years before the artists arrived, was the primary cause, since property values began rising along the transit route before any galleries opened.$q$, $q$Which choice best describes a key difference between the two historians' views?$q$, $q$["Historian A points to a cultural shift, while Historian B points to a prior infrastructure change.","Both historians agree that property values rose only after galleries opened.","Historian B argues that artists caused the neighborhood's transformation.","Historian A claims that the transit line was built after the galleries opened."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Historian A credits the arrival of artists and galleries, while Historian B credits a transit line built years earlier, noting values rose 'before any galleries opened' — the contrast in choice A.$q$),
  ($q$cs-220$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 1, $q$Text 1: Agricultural scientist A argues that vertical farms will soon outcompete traditional farms for leafy greens, citing their drastically lower water use and year-round growing seasons.

Text 2: Agricultural scientist B argues that vertical farms remain far more expensive per crop due to high energy costs for artificial lighting, limiting them to premium markets for now.$q$, $q$Based on the texts, how would Agricultural scientist B most likely respond to Agricultural scientist A's claim?$q$, $q$["By pointing to high energy costs as a barrier that limits vertical farming's near-term competitiveness","By agreeing that vertical farms will soon replace traditional farms entirely","By arguing that vertical farms use more water than traditional farms","By claiming that lighting costs do not affect vertical farming's expenses"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Agricultural scientist B's position is that high energy costs limit vertical farms 'to premium markets for now,' directly countering A's claim that they will soon outcompete traditional farms.$q$),
  ($q$cs-221$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 2, $q$Text 1: Musicologist A argues that a folk music genre's distinctive rhythm spread through oral tradition passed between traveling musicians at regional festivals.

